The predictions of the Smagorinsky model for the subgrid dissipation rate is shown as well. It is computed as  
\begin{equation}\label{eq:subgrid_diss_smag}
	{\varepsilon}_{sgs,SMAG} = 2 \left(C_s \Delta_s \right)^2 |\overline{{S}}|^3,
\end{equation}
where $C_s (=0.17)$ is the Smagorinsky constant, $\Delta_s=4\Delta_{DNS}$ is  the effective filter width, and $|\overline{{S}}|= 2\sqrt{\overline{{S}}_{ij}\,\overline{{S}}_{ij}}$ is the modulus of the filtered strain rate. It can be seen in  Fig.\,\ref{fig:01004-00H_PFT-Prt_ArchTest_condSRGANModel/inference_testOnfx/test4_4PDF_eps_sgs__x_gt_with_particle128_720000} that backscatter (negative subgrid dissipation)~\cite{PiomelliU1991_PFA} exists in the DNS which is well reproduced in the SR fields, however, the Smagorinsky model is by definition positive and cannot predict the backscatter.   Furthermore, the predicted positive subgrid dissipation rate from the SR fields match the DNS values better than the Smagorinsky model. 

