With $\sigma = 0.2$, Fig.~\ref{newCase:eb_0_2} reveals that the exercise region initially has a single boundary. At time $t^*  \approx 0.1 < t_1$, a second (lower) boundary emerges from zero, and both are increasing as time progresses. Similar behavior is observed for $\sigma = 0.4$ in Fig.~\ref{newCase:eb_0_4}, though here the upper and lower boundaries come closer together at $t \approx 0.8$ compared to the case shown in Fig.\ref{newCase:eb_0_2}. When $\sigma$ is further increased to 0.5087, as shown in Fig.~\ref{newCase:eb_0_5087}, the exercise boundaries intersect at $t \approx 0.82$. At this point, the original exercise region collapses, and simultaneously, a new exercise region forms with two boundaries for $t > 0.82$.

