It is also important to mention, that solving \eqref{Vol21-2} requires a very careful numerical procedure because otherwise it could converge to a different result due to a bad initial guess. In our calculations we used Matlab's \verb+fsolve+ function with a tolerance of $10^{-15}$ to solve this system of equations backward in time starting from $t = T$, as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Solve the first equation in \eqref{Vol21-2} for $X^{**}(t)$ using the initial guess $X^{**}(t+\Delta t)$ and setting $X^{*}(t) = X^{*}(t + \Delta t)$, to get $X^{**}_1(t)$.

\item Solve the second equation in \eqref{Vol21-2} for $X^{*}(t)$ using the initial guess $X^{*}(t+\Delta t)$ and setting $X^{**}(t) = X^{**}(t + \Delta t)$, to get $X^{*}_1(t)$.

\item Solve both equations in \eqref{Vol21-2} together using the initial guess $[\alpha X^{**}_1(t), \beta X^{*}_1(t)$ to get the final solution at time $t$. Here $\alpha = \beta = 1$ if either of $X^{**}_1(t), X^{*}_1(t)$ is very small. If not, we set  $\alpha = 1.05, \beta = 0.95$ if $X^{**}_1(t) > X^{*}_1(t)$, and $\alpha = 0.95, \beta = 1.05$ otherwise.

\end{enumerate}
The first two step of this procedure are needed to determine a good initial guess for the third step.

