	\begin{abstract}
We study the problem of signal source localization using bearing-only measurements. Initially, we present easily verifiable geometric conditions for sensor deployment to ensure the asymptotic identifiability of the model and demonstrate the consistency and asymptotic efficiency of the maximum likelihood (ML) estimator. However, obtaining the ML estimator is challenging due to its association with a non-convex optimization problem.
To address this, we propose a two-step estimator that shares the same asymptotic properties as the ML estimator while offering low computational complexity, linear in the number of measurements. The primary challenge lies in obtaining a preliminary consistent estimator in the first step. To achieve this, we construct a linear least-squares problem through algebraic operations on the measurement nonlinear model to first obtain a biased closed-form solution. We then eliminate the bias using the data to yield an asymptotically unbiased and consistent estimator. The key to this process is obtaining a consistent estimator of the variance of the sine of the noise by taking the reciprocal of the maximum eigenvalue of a specially constructed matrix from the data.
In the second step, we perform a single Gauss-Newton iteration using the preliminary consistent estimator as the initial value, achieving the same asymptotic properties as the ML estimator. Finally, simulation results demonstrate the superior performance of the proposed two-step estimator for large sample sizes.
	\end{abstract}

\keywords{Bearing-only measurements, Asymptotic identifiability, Maximum likelihood estimation,  Asymptotic efficiency, Two-step
estimators}

\section{Introduction}


 Signal source localization involves determining the source of a signal using measurements collected by sensors. This technique plays a crucial role in diverse fields such as acoustics \cite{Kundu2014, Canclini2013}, radar \cite{Mateusz2012}, seismic monitoring \cite{Dziewonski1981}, wireless communications \cite{He2022}, medical imaging \cite{Jatoi2014}, and navigation \cite{Li2024}, where pinpointing the location of a signal source is essential. The methods used for signal source localization vary based on the signal's nature, the surrounding environment, and the sensors involved. Generally, these methods are categorized according to the types of measurements they utilize, which include time of arrival (TOA) \cite{Chan1994,Soares2015,Han2016,Zeng2022}, time difference of arrival (TDOA) \cite{Ho2012,Sun2019,Zeng2024}, received signal strength (RSS) \cite{So2011,Angjelichinoski2015,Liu2016}, and angle of arrival (AOA) \cite{Ho2006, Dogancay2006,Wang2012, Shao2014,Wang2015,Han2016,Zheng2019, Sun2020, Wang2023}. Some methods integrate distinct types of measurements, such as AOA-TDOA \cite{Shen2010,Wang2018, Liu2019}.
	The AOA localization, which employs bearing-only measurements to determine the location of the signal source, does not require sensor synchronization in comparison with TOA and TDOA \cite{Wang2015, Wang2018}. Additionally, AOA can be precisely determined using antenna arrays, leading to accurate localization \cite{Wang2023}. In an AOA-based sensor network, each sensor independently measures the angles, reducing the demand for extensive data communication among sensors \cite{Wang2018}. These advantages make AOA-based localization methods particularly suitable for use with commercial off-the-shelf Wi-Fi platforms \cite{Zheng2019}. Therefore, this paper focuses on signal source localization using bearing-only measurements.

	The AOA localization identifies the source by intersecting rays derived from angle measurements. This differs from TOA localization, which determines the source location by intersecting circles or spheres based on range measurements, and TDOA localization, which determines the source location by intersecting hyperbolas or hyperboloids using range-difference measurements. In the absence of noises, these rays intersect precisely at a single point unless the source and all sensors are collinear. However, in practical scenarios, measurements are usually corrupted by noise, and the source coordinates must be deduced from noisy data.
	Therefore, AOA source localization is a typical parameter estimation problem. The most commonly utilized methods for addressing this problem are maximum likelihood (ML) and least squares (LS) methods, which are equivalent when the measurement noises are independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.) Gaussian random variables. The non-convex nature of the AOA localization problem poses a challenge in searching for a global minimum. To tackle this problem, two primary strategies emerge: iterative and relaxation-based methods.
	The iteration-based methods include:
	(i)
	Wang and Ho \cite{Wang2018} represent the source location in modified polar coordinates and apply the Gauss-Newton (GN) algorithm to search for the ML estimator with a priori knowledge that the source is in the near field. Wang \textit{et al.} \cite{Wang2018b} apply a similar technique in the 2-D scenario.
	(ii) Wang \textit{et al.} \cite{Wang2012} develop the location-penalized likelihood function in the 2-D scenario and apply the Broyden-Fletcher-Goldfarb-Shanno algorithm to maximize this penalized likelihood.
	These iteration-based methods, however, are computationally demanding and cannot guarantee finding the global maximum of the likelihood.
	Relaxation-based methods are mainly based on auxiliary linear regression models:
	(i)
	The sine and cosine functions involved in AOA localization are approximated by their first-order Taylor expansion, deriving approximate linear regression problems to address the AOA localization problem \cite{Wang2015, Ho2006, Dogancay2006, Sun2020, Wang2023}.
	(ii) A constrained weighted least-squares (CWLS) problem is constructed, and the source coordinates are inferred by relaxing the CWLS problem into a semi-definite programming (SDP) problem \cite{Sun2022, Chen2021}.
	It is worth noting that relaxation-based methods using SDP are often used to provide initial values for iteration-based methods \cite{Wang2018, Wang2018b}. Note that solving the SDP problem is still computationally demanding \cite{Sun2020,Wang2023}, and each iteration of the estimators proposed in \cite{Wang2018, Wang2018b} has a computational complexity of $\mathcal{O}(n^2)$, where $n$ represents the sample size.

	Since relaxed optimization problems may not be equivalent to the original one, relaxation-based methods tend to produce estimators with large biases that may not vanish even as the number of measurements increases to infinity \cite{Wang2015, Ho2006}. To mitigate this issue, three types of methods have been developed in the literature:
	(i)
	The first method isolates the noisy component from the regressor, treats this noisy part as a non-zero constant, and reformulates the problem as a CWLS problem \cite{Wang2015, Ho2006, Wang2023, Sun2020}. 
	Note that  the estimator proposed in \cite{Sun2020} has a computational complexity of $\mathcal{O}(n^3)$  and   the estimator proposed  in \cite{Wang2015} has a computational complexity of $\mathcal{O}(n)$  under uncorrelated noise condition.
	(ii)
	The second method  separates the noisy part from the regressor but focuses on minimizing the Frobenius norm of the error matrix with constraints on the relaxed linear model to derive a constrained total least-squares estimator \cite{Dogancay2005, Wang2007}.
	(iii)
	The third method approximates the bias and then compensates for it directly \cite{Dogancay2006, Shao2014, Sun2022, Chen2021}. Note that the estimator proposed in \cite{Chen2021} requires solving the computationally demanding SDP problem as well.
	Additionally, Luo \textit{et al.} \cite{Luo2019} avoid the common linearization method and instead use all bearing and intersensor bearing geometric information to construct a linear model insensitive to relaxation errors. However, these methods still rely on the approximation of the sine and cosine of noisy measurements, leading to potentially inaccurate models. Consequently, such estimators may not accurately recover the source location, and asymptotic efficiency cannot be guaranteed without imposing additional conditions. For instance, the bias-reduced estimator developed in \cite{Wang2015} achieves the Cramer-Rao lower bound (CRLB) in low noise conditions.
	To the best of our knowledge, there seem no existing methods that
	can globally converge to the true source at a certain rate and 
	achieve the same asymptotic properties as the ML estimator in AOA localization with a theoretical guarantee. 

	In this paper, we aim to present dual results on AOA localization: (i) establish the consistency and asymptotic efficiency of the ML estimator under readily verifiable conditions on sensor deployment and measurement noise; (ii) derive a two-step estimator that exhibits the same asymptotic properties as the ML estimator with computational complexity linear in the number of measurements.
	``Consistency" refers to the estimator converging to the true value as the number of measurements approaches infinity, while ``asymptotic efficiency" indicates that as the number of measurements increases, the mean squared error (MSE) approaches the CRLB—the theoretical minimum MSE for any unbiased estimator.
	Specifically, we establish easily verifiable geometric conditions for sensor deployment, such as non-collinearity, ensuring the identifiability of the AOA localization problem combined with conditions on measurement noises. Then, we derive the consistency and asymptotic normality of the ML estimator. 
	However, despite its favorable asymptotic properties, the ML estimator is challenging to solve accurately and efficiently.
	Therefore, we further prove that the negative log-likelihood function uniformly converges to a convex function in a local region around the true source location, termed the ``attraction region" \cite{Zeng2024}, where the true source location uniquely minimizes this function. Based on this result, we propose a two-step estimator with linear computational complexity in the number of measurements. This estimator achieves consistency and asymptotic efficiency comparable to the ML estimator for AOA localization.
	Specifically, our two-step process, previously introduced in \cite{Mu2017} for nonlinear rational system identification and successfully applied to TOA and TDOA \cite{Zeng2022, Zeng2024}, involves: (i) deriving an unbiased and globally consistent estimator of the source location; (ii) performing a single GN iteration on the consistent estimator obtained in step (i).
	The two-step estimator exhibits an attractive convergence property \cite{Lehmann1998}: it is consistent and asymptotically efficient provided the consistent estimator obtained in step (i) converges at a rate of $O_p(1/\sqrt{n})$.
	Thus, the crux of addressing AOA localization lies in deriving a globally consistent estimator for the true source location with a convergence rate of $O_p(1/\sqrt{n})$, achieved through the bias-eliminated least-squares (BELS) estimator proposed in this paper.

	The measurement model of the AOA localization problem, which combines arctangent and rational functions, exhibits stronger nonlinearity compared to the rational model, TOA, and TDOA models. This presents a significant challenge in deriving a globally consistent estimator for AOA localization.
	The globally consistent BELS estimator is derived through the following successive steps:
	(i)
	Initial linear regression model: We transform the original measurement model by taking the tangent of both sides to obtain an equivalent linear regression model with the true source as parameters. The LS estimator of this model is biased due to the correlation between regressors and noises but serves as the starting point for the BELS estimator.
	(ii)
	New linear regression model: We introduce a new linear regression model with the source as parameters, replacing the measurement output with noise-free output. We prove that the LS estimator of this new model is asymptotically unbiased and globally consistent with a convergence rate of \( O_p(1/\sqrt{n}) \). However, this LS estimator is impractical as its regressors involve unavailable noise-free outputs.
	(iii)
	Bias analysis: We analyze the forms of these two LS estimators, establish the explicit bias of the LS estimator of the initial model relative to the new one, and find that the bias asymptotically depends only on the variance of the sine of the noise and the available vector of averaged sensor coordinates.
	(iv)
	Consistent estimator for the variance of the sine of the noise: We construct a matrix from available data and prove that the reciprocal of its largest eigenvalue provides a globally consistent estimator for the variance of the sine of the noise, with a convergence rate of \( O_p(1/\sqrt{n}) \).
	(v)
	The BELS estimator: We replace the unknown variance of the sine of the noise involved in the bias with the consistent estimator, subtract the estimated bias from the LS estimator of the initial model, and derive the BELS estimator. We prove the BELS estimator is a globally consistent estimator of the true source with a convergence rate of \( O_p(1/\sqrt{n}) \).

