	The asymptotic and computational efficiency of the proposed two-step estimator guarantees that it achieves the highest possible estimation accuracy with scalable and low computational resources as the number of measurements approaches infinity. Localization using massive AOA measurements finds application in various practical scenarios. For instance, satellite constellations may comprise up to 40,000 satellites, each equipped with spaceborne interferometers. These devices are favored for their simple design, ease of installation, and low production costs \cite{Li2022}. Such constellations can provide a vast quantity of AOA measurements for geolocation purposes. Moreover, advancements in massive multiple-input multiple-output (MIMO) radar technology, particularly through millimeter-wave virtual MIMO techniques, enable rapid and precise AOA estimation \cite{Godrich2010,Li2022b}. This capability allows for accurate localization of sources moving at slower speeds compared to the rapid rate at which measurements are acquired.

	We summarize the contributions of this paper as follows:
	\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
		\item We provide practical conditions on sensor geometric deployment and measurement noise, ensuring the asymptotic identifiability of the AOA localization model. Specifically, for three-dimensional (3-D) localization problems, AOA only requires the noncollinearity of sensor deployment, unlike TOA, which requires noncoplanarity, and TDOA, which requires either noncoplanarity or nonhyperboloid conditions. Additionally, we establish the consistency and asymptotic efficiency of the ML estimator under these conditions.

		\item We introduce a two-step estimator for the AOA localization problem, comprising the BELS estimator followed by a single GN iteration. We establish the global consistency and asymptotic efficiency of this two-step estimator, which holds  asymptotic properties without restrictions on noise intensity.

		\item We demonstrate that the two-step estimator exhibits linear computational complexity relative to the number of measurements. This feature makes the estimator particularly suitable for real-time applications.
	\end{enumerate}

	The rest of the paper is organized as follows. Section \ref{sec2} addresses the two-dimensional (2-D) AOA localization problem, discussing the asymptotic identifiability, the asymptotic efficiency of the ML estimator, and the two-step estimator. Section \ref{sec3} extends the 2-D AOA localization to the 3-D case. Section \ref{sec4} demonstrates the estimation accuracy and computational efficiency of the estimator using extensive Monte-Carlo simulations. Finally, Section \ref{sec5} concludes the paper.

