		\subsection{2-D scenario: random sensors}
		We consider the  random sensor deployment in this subsection. The source's coordinates are $p^0=[150,0]^T$. The number of sensors is set to be $n=100,300,1000,2000,3000$, and $5000$, respectively. For each choice of $n$, we randomly generate $n$ sensors $\{p_i=[100\cos(\beta_i),~ 100\sin(\beta_i)]^T,~i=1,...,n\}$ where $\{\beta_i,~i=1,...,n\}$ are independently sampled from the uniform distribution on $[0,2\pi]$. This is equivalent to that the sensors are uniformly distributed on the circle with radius $100$ and center $[0,0]^T$. Thus, it is straightforward to check that Assumptions \ref{assum_coordinates_2d}-\ref{assum_colinearity} hold. The noise is generated under Assumption \ref{assum_mea_noise_2d} with $\sigma_a=0.2$, and each sensor makes one observation. 

		We exhibit the biases and RMSEs for varying sample sizes (varying $n$, equivalently) of every estimator. For each $n$, 1000 Monte-Carlo experiments are conducted. Notice that the subspace estimator requires geometric information of every pair of sensors with individual locations, whose time complexity grows rapidly with the number of sensors' individual locations. Thus, we do not include the subspace estimator in this comparison for its high time complexity. 

