		\subsection{3-D scenario: fixed and coplanar sensors}
		In the 3-D scenario, both TOA and TDOA localization methods require noncoplanar sensor deployment to ensure the asymptotic identifiability of the true source. In contrast, the AOA localization only requires noncollinear sensor deployment. Therefore, in this subsection, we specifically verify that  the sensors can be coplanar for the AOA localization. We fix 10 sensors on the plane $z=0$ with the following coordinates:
		\begin{align*}
			& p_1 = [0,100,0]^T,~p_2=[0,50,0]^T,~p_3=[50,50,0]^T,~p_4=[50,0,0]^T,~p_5=[50,-50,0]^T,\\
			& p_6 = [0,-50,0]^T,~p_7=[0,-100,0]^T,~p_8=[-50,-50,0]^T,~p_9=[-50,0,0]^T,~p_{10}=[-50,50,0]^T.
		\end{align*}
		Each sensor performs $T$ independent observations. We consider two cases: in the first case, the true source is $p^0=[60,10,10]^T$, which is noncoplanar with the sensors. In the second case, the true source is $p^0=[60,10,0]^T$, coplanar with the sensors. It is straightforward to verify that Assumptions \ref{assum_coordinates_3d} and \ref{assum_projection} are satisfied for both cases.

		We analyze the biases and RMSEs of each estimator for varying sample sizes (varying $T$) in both cases. For both cases, the azimuth and elevation angle measurement noises are generated according to Assumption \ref{assum_mea_noise_3d} with $\sigma_a = \sigma_e = 0.2$. For each $T$, we conduct 1000 Monte-Carlo experiments. The biases for both cases are shown in Fig. \ref{fig:3d_coplanar_bias}. Similar to the noncoplanar scenario, the PLS estimator shows a large bias. The MPR-SDP+GN estimator has a smaller bias but remains biased.
		In contrast, the BR-PLE, MPR-EV, Bias-Eli and Bias-Eli+GN estimators are asymptotically unbiased.
		The RMSEs of the estimators for both cases are shown in Fig. \ref{fig:3d_coplanar_rmse}. As expected, the PLS estimator produces the largest RMSE, while the MPR-SDP+GN estimator shows a smaller RMSE but converges to a nonzero constant due to its bias. The RMSEs of the BR-PLE and MPR-EV estimators approach zero but remain larger than the RCRLB. Note that in these specific scenarios, the approximation error associated with the elevation angle is smaller than it in the noncoplanar case, helping the BR-PLE estimator enhance its accuracy.
		The Bias-Eli+GN estimator, however, is asymptotically unbiased, consistent, and asymptotically efficient since its RMSE achieves the RCRLB. Thus, the AOA localization indeed does not require noncoplanar sensor deployment.


