\begin{abstract}
	The Bures-Wasserstein geometry of covariance matrices stands at the crossroad of statistics, information geometry, quantum information and optimal transport. The space of covariance matrices admits stratified structure with strata consisting on fixed rank covariance matrices. In this paper we focus on the rank-$k$ stratum $\Sym(n,k)$  and revisit its geometry through a new diffeomorphic model $\Sym(n,k)\cong\St(n,k)\times_{O(k)}\Sym^{+}(k)$. Working in this	bundle picture, we (i) prove that the fibers are totally geodesic, (ii) derive a system of differential equations for Bures–Wasserstein geodesics, (iii) exhibit several examples of closed-form solutions, and (iv) prove a one-to-one correspondence between Grassmannian and $\Sym(n,k)$ logarithms and hence minimizing geodesics.  The alternative	approach we propose, clarifies the role of the underlying base $\Gr(k,n)$ and sets the stage for further investigations into low-rank covariance matrices.
\end{abstract}

\textbf{Keywords}: fixed rank covariance matrices, Bures-Wasserstein, Grassmannian,
geodesics, fiber bundle. 


	\section{Introduction}
	Covariance matrices are present and vastly utilized in many fields of study including statistics, physics and engineering as they can represent many different data.  Their intrinsic geometry under
	the Bures–Wasserstein metric is central for information geometry and
	optimal transport.  A deep geometric
	understanding therefore translates into stronger theoretical guarantees
	and more efficient algorithms across all applications. In particular, recent statistical literature has tapped into this geometrical framework to develop estimation algorithms for covariance matrices (\cite{chandrasekaran2010latent}, \cite{bernardi2022log}). The smooth manifold of fixed rank covariance matrix is very relevant in applications as well. Interpolation in this manifold is used in \cite{massart2019interpolation} for parametric order reduction and in \cite{bakker2018dynamic} for community detection. Optimization algorithms for various problems are proposed in \cite{bonnabel2010adaptive}, \cite{meyer2011regression} (distance learning), \cite{marchand2016riemannian} (distance matrix completion) and \cite{mishra2011low} (role model extraction). Other studies have tackled the task of fitting a curve to points on the manifold.
	In \cite{gousenbourger2017piecewise} the authors fit a curve through covariance matrices that model a wind field. In a related vein, in \cite{li2014conformational} the authors use geodesic interpolation between Gram matrices to create intermediate protein conformations, while reference \cite{kacem2018novel} employs Gram-matrix interpolation to track facial-expression changes in video data.

	The set of $n$-dimensional covariance matrices $\Cov(n)$ is defined as the set of  positive semidefinite symmetric matrices
	\begin{equation*}
		\Cov(n):=\left\{\Sigma \in \Sym(n) \, \vert\, \Sigma \succeq 0\right\},
	\end{equation*}
	where $\Sym(n)$ denotes the space of symmetric $n \times n $ matrices. The set $\Cov(n)$ forms a closed convex cone in the Euclidean space of square $n$-dimensional matrices $\Mat(n)$. This set can be viewed as a stratified space whose strata are given by the smooth manifolds of $n$-dimensional covariance matrix of fixed rank $k$ (see \cite{thanwerdas2023bures})
	\begin{equation*}
		\Sym(n,k):=\left\{\Sigma \in \Cov(n) \, \vert\, \rk(\Sigma)=k\right\}.
	\end{equation*}
	The set of $n$-dimensional covariance matrices can be identified with the quotient space $\Mat(n) /O(n)$, where $O(n)$ denotes the orthogonal group. The orbits of the quotient space are defined by the right action $\Mat(n) \times O(n) \ni (X,G) \mapsto XG \in \Mat(n)$ and the identification is given by the map 
	\begin{equation}
		\label{int. identification map}
		\Mat(n) \ni X \mapsto X X^T \in \Cov(n).
	\end{equation}
	This is the approach that the recent literature has undertaken in order to study the space $\Cov(n)$ and its strata. The purpose of this work is to gain new insights on the Riemannian
	manifold $(\Sym(n,k),d^{BW})$ of symmetric positive semidefinite (PSD)
	matrices of fixed rank $k$, where $d^{BW}$ denotes the
	Bures–Wasserstein distance, by changing the geometrical perspective and
	studying the diffeomorphism
	\begin{equation*}
		\Sym(n,k)\;\cong\;
		M(n,k):=\St(n,k)\times_{O(k)}\Sym^{+}(k)
		=(\St(n,k)\times\Sym^{+}(k))/O(k),
	\end{equation*}
	where $\St(n,k)$ denotes the Stiefel manifold and $M(n,k)$ the bundle associated
	to the principal $O(k)$-bundle
	$\pi_G\colon\St(n,k)\to\Gr(k,n)$. Utilizing this novel approach we are able to obtain a system of geodesic differential equations, show that the fibers are totally geodesic and highlight the important role of the base manifold $\Gr(k,n)$ in describing the geometry of $\Sym(n,k)$. In particular, we find that the energy minimizing geodesics in $\Sym(n,k)$ have a one-to-one correspondence with the ones in $\Gr(k,n)$.

	The Bures-Wasserstein distance, with which we equip the manifold of study, is full of applications and of great interest in the literature. It was introduced both in quantum information theory as the Bures distance (\cite{bures1969extension}, \cite{helstrom1967minimum}) and in optimal transport as the $L^2$-Wasserstein distance (\cite{dowson1982frechet}, \cite{olkin1982distance}).
	In particular in the latter context, the distance acquires the meaning of measure of dissimilarity between Gaussian distributions (\cite{malago2018wasserstein}, \cite{villani2008optimal}). From a geometric point of view, the Bures-Wasserstein distance can be derived as the quotient distance via the map (\ref{int. identification map}) if we assign the Euclidean (or Frobenius) distance to $\Mat(n)$.
	It is defined as
	\begin{equation*}
		d^{BW}(\Sigma,\Lambda)^2=\Tr(\Sigma)+\Tr(\Lambda)-2\Tr((\Sigma^{1/2}\Lambda\Sigma^{1/2})^{1/2}).
	\end{equation*}
	With this distance, $\Cov(n)$ becomes a complete geodesic space (i.e. a complete metric space where the distance between two points is equal to the length of a minimizing geodesic connecting the two points) of nonnegative curvature (\cite{thanwerdas2023bures}, \cite{takatsu2011wasserstein}).

	For a stratum $\Sym(n,k)$ a parallel construction to the one of $\Cov(n)$ can be built (\cite{massart2020quotient}). In this case, we focus on the right action $\Mat(n,k)^\ast \times O(k) \ni (X,G) \mapsto XG \in \Sym(n,k)$, where $\Mat(n,k)^\ast$ denotes the manifold of full rank $n \times k$ matrices, and the consequent quotient space $\Mat(n,k)^\ast/O(k)$ which is diffeomorphic to $\Sym(n,k)$ via the map $\Mat(n,k)^\ast \ni X \mapsto X X^T \in \Sym(n,k)$. Thanks to this identification, the Euclidean metric descends to the Bures-Wasserstein Riemannian metric on $\Sym(n,k)$. For a more in-depth analysis we refer the reader to \cite{massart2020quotient}.

	Before defining the metric, we recall the definition of the tangent space to $\Sym(n,k)$ at $\Sigma$
	\begin{equation*}
		T_\Sigma = \{V \in \Sym(n) \,|\,P_\Sigma^\perp V P_\Sigma^\perp=0\},
	\end{equation*}
	where $P_\Sigma^\perp$ denotes the orthogonal projectors onto the nullspace of $\Sigma$.
	Now, following what is done in \cite{thanwerdas2023bures}, we set for a moment $\Sigma=Q D Q^T$ with $D=\diag(d_1,...,d_k)$ positive definite and $Q \in \St(n,k)$ and call $S_{\Sigma,V}=Q S_D(Q^T V Q) Q^T$, where $S_A(B)=:S$ denotes the unique solution to the Sylvester equation $B=AS+SA$ for a symmetric positive definite matrix $A$ and a symmetric matrix $B$. For $V, \, W \in T_\Sigma$, the Bures-Wasserstein metric is given by
	\begin{align*}
		g_\Sigma^{BW(n,k)}(V,W)=&\Tr( S_{\Sigma,V} \Sigma S_{\Sigma,W}) + \Tr(P_\Sigma^\perp V \Sigma^\dagger W )\\=:&	g_\Sigma^{\hor}(V,W)+	g_\Sigma^{\ver}(V,W),
	\end{align*}
	where $\Sigma^\dagger$ denotes the Moore-Penrose inverse of $\Sigma$. Furthermore, the authors point out that $S_{\Sigma,W}$ and the metric are independent of the chosen decomposition.


	With the above geometry constructions, the strata of $\Cov(n)$ have been object of study in the litearure for some years. The first results concerned the full rank principal stratum (see among others \cite{bhatia2019bures}, \cite{malago2018wasserstein}, \cite{takatsu2010wasserstein}, \cite{takatsu2011wasserstein}, \cite{thanwerdas2023n}), where starting from the quotient geometry it was possible to derive equations for the exponential map (\cite{malago2018wasserstein}), unique logarithm (\cite{bhatia2019bures}, \cite{massart2020quotient}), parallel transport (\cite{thanwerdas2023n}) and curvature (\cite{takatsu2010wasserstein}, \cite{takatsu2011wasserstein}, \cite{thanwerdas2023n}). For the geometry of the stratum $\Sym(n,k)$ of fixed rank covariance matrices we refer to \cite{massart2020quotient}, \cite{massart2019curvature}, \cite{thanwerdas2023bures}; in particular the quotient geometry was studied in \cite{massart2020quotient}, a formula for the curvature tensor and the sectional curvature, as well as curvature bounds, were expressed, thanks to O'Neill's equations (\cite{o1966fundamental}), in \cite{massart2019curvature}, and finally the exponential map, its definition domain, cut time and logarithms together with their multiplicity were found in \cite{thanwerdas2023bures}. Most recently, we gained some insights on the geometry (and in particular on the geodesics) of the stratified space as a whole (\cite{thanwerdas2023bures}).



	As noted above, with our novel geometric approach we are able to prove that the fibers are totally geodesic and obtain a system of geodesic differential equations in $M(n,k)$ that are seen in some example cases to give close form geodesics that pull back to the known geodesic formulas on $\Sym(n,k)$. Moreover, we find a bijection between the index set of logarithms in the Grassmannian $Gr(k,n)$ endowed with the Euclidean metric (see \cite{bendokat2024grassmann}) and the index set of logarithms in $\Sym(n,k)$ provided in \cite{thanwerdas2023bures}.

	The hope for the future is to continue to study this alternative perspective in order to gain new insights on some of the unanswered geometric questions regarding the set of covariance matrices as a whole and its strata.


	The article is organized as follows.
	In Section \ref{sec. preliminaries}, we introduce the needed geometrical framework. In particular, in Subsection \ref{subsec. fiber bundles} we introduce some definitions and useful results of differential geometry, while in Subsection \ref{subsec. principal bundle} we describe the principal fiber bundle $\pi_G: \St(n,k) \to \Gr(k,n)$.  

	In Section \ref{sec. sym geometry}, we get into the heart of the topic and study the geometry of the stratum $\Sym(n,k)$. In Subsection \ref{subsec. diffeomoprhism}, we introduce and prove the diffeomoprhism $\Sym(n,k) \cong M(n,k)$; in Subsection \ref{subsec. tangent spaces} we give a characterization of the tangent spaces to $M(n,k)$; in Subsection \ref{subsec. metric on sym}, we study how the Bures-Wasserstein metric on $\Sym(n,k)$ translates to a metric on $M(n,k)$; in Subsection \ref{subsec. geodesics}, we analyze the geodesics in $M(n,k)$ and find a system of geodesic equations and finally in Subsection \ref{subsec. bijection} a bijection between the logarithms of $\Sym(n,k)$ and $\Gr(k,n)$ is proved.



	\section{Preliminaries}
	\label{sec. preliminaries}
	\subsection{Fiber bundles}
	\label{subsec. fiber bundles}
	Here we introduce the needed concepts from differential geometry. For the interested reader we refer to \cite{sharpe2000differential} and \cite{husemoller1966fibre} for a more comprehensive approach to the matter. We define fiber bundles, principal bundles and associated fiber bundles.
	\begin{definition}
		Let $E, \, B$ and $F$ be smooth manifolds and $\pi:E \to B$ a smooth surjection. The quadruple $(E,B,\pi,F)$ si called a \textit{smooth fiber bundle} if for every $b \in B$ there exists a neighbourhood $U \subset B$ and a diffeomorphism $\psi_U: \pi^{-1}(U) \to U \times F$ such that the following diagram commutes
		\begin{equation*}
			\begin{tikzcd}
				 \pi^{-1}(U) \arrow{r}{\psi} \arrow[swap]{dr}{\pi} & U \times F \arrow{d}{\pr_1} \\
				& U
			\end{tikzcd}
		\end{equation*}
		where $\pr_1$ denotes the canonical projection onto $U$. $B$ is called \textit{base space}, $E$ \textit{total space} and $\pi$ \textit{projection}; for $b \in B$, $E_b:=\pi^{-1}(b)$ is diffeomorphic to $F$ and is called \textit{fiber}. The family $\{(U,\psi_U)\}$ is called a (smooth) \textit{local trivialization}.
		Fiber bundles can be denoted simply by $(E,\pi)$ or $\pi: E \to B$.
	\end{definition}
	\begin{definition}
		Let $\pi_1: E_1 \to B$ and $\pi_2 : E_2 \to B$ be two fiber bundles over the same base space. A smooth map $\phi:E_1 \to E_2$ is called a \textit{bundle morphism} if it respects the projections, i.e.,
		\begin{equation*}
			\pi_1= \pi_2 \circ \phi.
		\end{equation*}
	\end{definition}
	We observe that $\phi$ takes every fiber $\pi_1^{-1}(b)$ in $E_1$ into the corresponding fiber $\pi_2^{-1}(b)$ in $E_2$.
	\begin{definition}
		A bundle morphism $\phi: E_1 \to E_2$ is called an \textit{isomorphism between fiber bundles} or a \textit{bundle isomorphism} if it is a diffeomorphism. The two bundles $\pi_1: E_1 \to B$ and $\pi_2 : E_2 \to B$ are called \textit{isomorphic}.
	\end{definition}
	\begin{definition}
		Let $G$ be a Lie group. We call \textit{principal} $G$-\textit{bundle} a fiber bundle $\pi:P \to B$ to which is associated a right action $R_g: P \to P, \, x \to R_g x=xg$ that satisfies the following properties.
		\begin{itemize}
			\item[(i)] for each $g \in G$, $R_g$ is a diffeomorphism;
			\item[(ii)] the action preserves the fibers and is free and transitive on them, i.e., for every fiber $P_b=\pi^{-1}(b)$ and every pair of points $x,\, y \in P_b$ there exists a unique $g \in G$ such that $y=xg$.
		\end{itemize}
	\end{definition}
	The two properties imply that every fiber $P_b$ is diffeomorphic to $G$ and can be identified with $G$ itself.
	\begin{definition}
		Let $\pi:P \to B$ be a principal $G$-bundle. Let $F$ be a smooth manifold equipped with a left action by $G$ defined as $L_g: F \to F,\, x \to L_g x= g \cdot x$. Define a $G$ action on $P \times F$ as 
		\begin{equation*}
			(p,x)\cdot g=(pg, g^{-1}\cdot x).
		\end{equation*}
		Denote the total space as $E= P \times_G F := (P \times F)/G$. We call \textit{fiber bundle associated to the principal} $G$-\textit{bundle}, or \textit{associated} $G$-\textit{bundle}, the fiber bundle defined by the projection
		\begin{equation*}
			\Tilde{\pi}: E \to B, \quad [p,x]\mapsto \pi(p),
		\end{equation*}
		where $[p,x]$ denotes an equivalence class in $E$.
	\end{definition} 

	\begin{proposition}
		\label{prop. tangent spaces of associated bundle}
		 For a $P \to B$ principal $G$-bundle and an associated bundle $E=P \times_G F$ with fiber $F$ and left action $L_g f=g \cdot f$ the tangent spaces are given by 
		\begin{equation*}
			T_{[p,f]} E= (T_pP \times T_f F)/\{p \xi, -L_{\xi}^\ast r\,\vert\, \xi \in \mathfrak{g}\},
		\end{equation*}
		where $\mathfrak{g}$ is the Lie algebra of $G$ and $L_{\xi}^\ast:= \partial_t L_{\exp(t\xi)}\vert_{t=0}$.
	\end{proposition}
	\begin{proof}
		Let $q$ be the canonical projection $q: P \times F \to E$. By definition $q$ is a submersion and its differential $dq_{(p,f)}: T_p P \times T_f F \to T_{[p,f]} E$ is surjective with kernel corresponding to the tangent space to the $G$ orbit $\cl{O}_{(p,f)}$ through $(p,f)$. 

		For any $\xi \in \mathfrak{g}$ we consider the one parameter subgroup $\exp(t \xi)$ and the curve 
		\begin{align*}
			\gamma_\xi(t):=&(p,f) \cdot \exp(t \xi)\\=&
			(p \exp(t\xi), L_{\exp(-t\xi)}f)
		\end{align*} 
		which lies completely in $\cl{O}_{(p,f)}$. Differentiating at $t=0$ we get
		\begin{align*}
			\partial_t \gamma_\xi(t)\vert_{t=0}=(p \xi, -L_{\xi}^\ast f).
		\end{align*}
		Since  the set of curves $\{\gamma_\xi\,\vert \, \xi \in \mathfrak{g}\}$ exhausts all directions in the orbit, we get
		\begin{equation*}
			\Ker (d q_{(p,f)})=\{p \xi, -L_{\xi}^\ast r\,\vert\, \xi \in \mathfrak{g}\}.
		\end{equation*}
		Now, the claim follows from
		\begin{equation*}
			T_{[p,f]} E= (T_pP \times T_f F)/\Ker (d q_{(p,f)}).
		\end{equation*}
	\end{proof}

	\begin{lemma}
		\label{local to global diff}
		Let $\pi_M: M \to B, \, \pi_N: N \to B$ be two smooth fiber bundles over the same base space. Let $\phi: M \to N, \pi_N= \pi_M \circ \phi$ be a smooth bundle morphism such that the restriction on the fibers is a diffeomorphism. Then $\phi$ is a global diffeomorphism.
	\end{lemma}
	\begin{proof}
		First, we observe that $\phi$ is
		\begin{itemize}
			\item surjective; indeed for $y \in N$, $y=\phi_{b}(x)=\phi(x)$, where $\phi_b$ is the restriction of $\phi$ over the fiber $N_b$ for $b=\pi_N(y) \in B$.
			\item Injective, as, if $\phi(x_1)=\phi(x_2)$ the $\pi_M(x_1)=\pi_N(\phi(x_1))=\pi_N(\phi(x_2))=\pi_M(x_2)$ and hence $x_1=x_2$ since $\phi$ is a fiberwise diffeomoprhism.
		\end{itemize}
		Since $M$ and $N$ are smooth fiber bundles, there exists an open cover $\{U_\alpha\}$ of $B$ and smooth local trivialisations
		\begin{equation*}
			\psi_\alpha^M: \pi_M^{-1} (U_\alpha) \to U_\alpha \times F, \qquad \psi_\alpha^N: \pi_N^{-1} (U_\alpha) \to U_\alpha \times F,
		\end{equation*}
		where $F$ is a model fiber diffeomorphic to the fibers of both bundles. In this coordinates
		\begin{equation*}
			\tilde{\phi}_\alpha= \psi_\alpha^N \circ \phi\circ (\psi_\alpha^M)^{-1}: U_\alpha \times F \to U_\alpha \times F
		\end{equation*}
		takes the form 
		\begin{equation*}
			\tilde{\phi}_\alpha(b,x)=(b,f(b,x)),
		\end{equation*}
		where $f: U_\alpha \times N \to N$ is smooth and for every $b \in U_\alpha$, $f_b(\cdot):=f(b,\cdot)$ is a diffeomorphism. The differential of $\tilde{\phi}_\alpha$ is given by
		\begin{equation*}
			D\tilde{\phi}_\alpha=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
				I&0\\\ast&D_xf
			\end{array}\right]
		\end{equation*}
		so that $D\tilde{\phi}_\alpha \neq 0$ and hence $\tilde{\phi}_\alpha^{-1}(b,x)=(b,f_b^{-1}(x))$, is a local diffeomorphism that is one to one, a global (in the sense of $U_\alpha \times F$) diffeomorphism.
		This forces $\phi^{-1}$ to be smooth in every chart, and hence smooth globally.
	\end{proof}

	\subsection{The Grassmannian as a principal fiber bundle}
	\label{subsec. principal bundle}


	 We are now going to go through the most important informations about the well-known principal $O(k)$-bundle $\pi_G:\St(n,k) \to \Gr(k,n), \, Q \mapsto \Span(Q)$. For a comprehensive review of this topic we refer the viewer to \cite{bendokat2024grassmann}. Let $Q \in \St(n,k)$; the space tangent to $\St(n,k)$ at $Q$ can be characterized as
	\begin{equation*}
		T_Q \St(n,k)=\{V \in \Mat(n,k) \,\vert\, Q^TV + V^T Q=0\}.
	\end{equation*}
	We may uniquely decompose $V \in T_Q \St(n,k)$ as
	\begin{equation*}
		V=Q A + Q_\perp B, \quad A \in \mathfrak{o}(k)=\Skew(k), \, B \in \Mat(n-k,k),
	\end{equation*}
	where $Q_\perp$ completes $Q$ to an orthonormal basis of $\R^n$.
	We set the connection:
	\begin{itemize}
		\item vertical spaces: $\cl{V}_Q=\{QA \, \vert \, A \in \mathfrak{o}(k)\}$,
		\item horizontal spaces: $\cl{H}_Q=\{Q_\perp B \, \vert \, B \in \Mat(n-k,k) \}$.
	\end{itemize}
	This decomposition is orthogonal with respect to Frobenius metric.
	 We identify the space tangent to $\Gr(k,n)$ at $P$ as
	\begin{equation*}
		T_P \Gr(k,n)=\Hom(P, P^\perp).
	\end{equation*}
	Once a basis is specified, we can also identify $\Hom(P, P^\perp) \cong \Mat(n-k,k)$.

	\begin{proposition}
		We have $\Ker(d\pi_G(Q))=\cl{V}_Q.$
	\end{proposition}
	\begin{proof}
		For $A \in \mathfrak{o}(k)$ we consider the one-parameter subgroup $\exp(tA)\in O(k)$. Differentiating the curve $\gamma_Q^A(t):=Q\exp(tA) \in \St(n,k)$ at $t=0$ we get
		\begin{equation*}
			\Dot{\gamma}_Q^A(0)=QA.
		\end{equation*} 
		Furthermore, $\gamma_Q^A(t)$ stays inside a single fiber and its projection is constant. Hence, $d\pi_G(Q(t))\vert_{t=0}=0$, from which
		\begin{equation*}
			\cl{V}_Q \subset \Ker(d\pi_G(Q)).
		\end{equation*} 
		Since $\dim(\cl{V}_Q)= \dim(\Ker(d\pi_G(Q)))$ we have equality of the two subspaces.
	\end{proof}

	The standard construction is to endow both $\St(n,k)$ and $\Gr(k,n)$ with the Euclidean metric, i.e., 
	\begin{alignat}{2}
		\label{metric on grassmannian}
		g^{\St}(V_1,V_2)=\Tr(V_1 V_2^T)&, \quad && V_1 , V_2 \in T_Q \St(n,k), \nonumber \\
		g^{\Gr}(B_1,B_2)=\Tr(B_1 B_2^T)&, \quad && B_1, B_2 \in \Mat(n-k,k) \cong T_P \Gr(k,n),
	\end{alignat}
	where the Grassmannian metric is independent of the basis chosen for the matrix representatives. 
	\begin{proposition}
		$\pi_G$ is a riemannian submersion, i.e., $d\pi_G \vert_{\cl{H}_Q}$ is an isometry between $\cl{H}_Q$ and $T_P \Gr(k,n)$.
	\end{proposition}
	\begin{proof}
		Take an horizontal $V=Q_\perp B$ and a curve $Q(t) \in \St(n,k)$ with $Q(0)=Q$ and $\Dot{Q}(0)=V$. For any $x \in \R^{k}$ consider $v(t)=Q(t)x \in P(t):=\pi_G(Q(t))$. We have
		\begin{equation*}
			\Dot{v}(0)=Vx=Q_\perp B x.
		\end{equation*}
		Since $x \in \R^{k}$ was arbitrary, we get, in the basis $[Q\, Q_\perp]$ of $\R^n$
		\begin{equation}
			\label{diff of projection in principal bundle}
			d\pi_G(Q)(V)=B \in \Mat(n-k,k).
		\end{equation}
		The claim is now readily seen to be true.
	\end{proof}







	\section{The Bures-Wasserstein Geometry of the fixed rank stratum}
	\label{sec. sym geometry}
	\subsection{The diffeomorphism}
	\label{subsec. diffeomoprhism}



		We recall that, as described in Subsection \ref{subsec. principal bundle}, $\St(n,k)$ can be characterized as a principal fiber bundle with base space $\Gr(k,n)$ and structure group $O(k)$ acting by right multiplication. Indeed, the action is \textit{free} and \textit{proper} and the projection $\pi_G: \St(n,k) \to \Gr(k,n), \, \pi_G(Q)=\Span(Q)$ is a surjective submersion.

		We equip $\Sym^+(k)$ with the smooth left $O(k)$-action $G \cdot S=GS G^T$ and form the \textit{associated fiber bundle}
		\begin{equation*}
			M(n,k)=\St(n,k) \times_{O(k)}  \Sym^+(k) =\left(\St(n,k)\times \Sym^+(k)\right)/O(k)
		\end{equation*}
		where the equivalence relation is 
		\begin{equation*}
			(Q,S)\sim(Q G, G^T \cdot S)=(Q G, G^T S G)
		\end{equation*}
		and the equivalence classes are denoted, as usual, as $[Q,S]$. The projection of the associated bundle $\pi_M: M(n,k) \to \Gr(k,n)$ is defined by
		\begin{equation*}
			\pi_M([Q,S])=\pi_G(Q)=\Span(Q)
		\end{equation*}
		and the fibers are given by
		\begin{equation*}
			M_P(n,k)=\{[Q,S] \,\vert\, \Span(Q)=P\} 
		\end{equation*}
		for $P \in \Gr(k,n)$.

		We prove the following.
		\begin{proposition}
			Set $P \in \Gr(k,n)$. The fiber $M_P(n,k)$ is diffeomorphic to $\Sym^+(k)$
		\end{proposition}
		\begin{proof}
			Fix $Q_0 \in \St(n,k)$ that spans $P$. We define the map $\psi: \Sym^+(k) \to M_P, \, S \mapsto [Q_0,S]$. $\psi_2$ is:
			\begin{itemize}
				\item injective, as $[Q_0,S_1]=[Q_0,S_2]$ implies there exists a $G \in O(k)$ such that $Q_0=Q_0 G$ and  $S_2= G^T S_1 G$, which clearly gives $S_1=S_2$;
				\item surjective, since
				\begin{equation*}
					\{Q \in \St(n,k)\,\vert\, \Span(Q)=P\}=\{Q_0 G \,\vert\, G \in O(k)\}
				\end{equation*} 
				and
				\begin{equation*}
					[Q,S]=[Q_0G,S]=[Q_0,G S G^T]=\psi( G S G^T);
				\end{equation*}
				\item smooth, readily.
			\end{itemize}
			Set $\psi^{-1}: M_P(n,k) \to \Sym^+(k), \,[Q,S] \mapsto Q^T Q_0 S Q_0^T Q$. The map $\psi^{-1}$ is well-defined (invariant with respect to the $O(k)$ action) and we have
			\begin{gather*}
				\psi(\psi^{-1}([Q,S]))=[Q_0,Q^T Q_0 S Q_0^T Q]=[Q,S],\\
				\psi^{-1}(\psi(S))=S.
			\end{gather*}
			Thus, $\psi^{-1}$ is indeed the inverse of $\psi$. Finally, $\psi^{-1}$ is smooth as $V \mapsto V_0^T V$ and the canonical projection are smooth on the product manifold $\St(n,k) \times \Sym^+(k)$ and the map is invariant with respect to the action that gives the equivalence classes.
		\end{proof}

		 On the other hand, we define the bundle
		\begin{equation*}
			\pi_{\Sym}: \Sym(n,k) \to \Gr(k,n), \qquad \pi_{\Sym}(\Sigma)=\Imm(\Sigma).
		\end{equation*}
		The fibers are then given by
		\begin{equation*}
			\Sym_P(n,k)=\{\Sigma \in \Sym(n) \,\vert\, \Sigma \succeqq 0, \rk(\Sigma)=k, \Imm(\Sigma)=P\}
		\end{equation*}
		for $P \in \Gr(k,n)$. $\Sym(n,k)$ has a fiber bunde structure with same base space as $M$.

		We define the map $\phi: M(n,k) \to \Sym(n,k), \, \phi([Q,S])=Q S Q^T$. It is immediate that $\phi$ is well defined (invariant under $O(k)$ action) and we can check that it preserves fibers. Indeed
		\begin{equation*}
			\pi_{\Sym}(Q S Q^T)=\Span(Q)=\pi_{M}([Q,S]).
		\end{equation*}
		Hence $\phi$ is a smooth bundle morphism.


		\begin{proposition}
			The smooth bundle morphism $\phi$ is a fiberwise diffeomorphism.
		\end{proposition}
		\begin{proof}
			Take $P \in \Gr(k,n)$ and $Q_0 \in \St(n,k)$ such that $\Span(Q_0)=P$. For every $S \in \Sym^+(k)$ we take $(Q_0,S)\in[Q_0,S] \in M_P(n,k)$. Restricting $\phi$ on the fiber $M_P$ and recalling that $M_P(n,k) \cong \Sym^+(k)$, we get the map
			\begin{equation*}
				\phi_{M_P(n,k)}: \Sym^+(k) \to \Sym_P(n,k), \qquad S \mapsto Q_0 S Q_0^T
			\end{equation*} 
			which is clearly a diffeomorphism.
		\end{proof}

		We can now apply Lemma \ref{local to global diff} to $\phi: M(n,k) \xrightarrow{\cong}\Sym(n,k)$ to prove the following.
		 \begin{theorem}
		 	Let
		 	\begin{equation*}
		 		M(n,k)=\St(n,k) \times_{O(k)}  \Sym^+(k) =\left(\St(n,k)\times \Sym^+(k)\right)/O(k).
			\end{equation*}
		 	Then $M(n,k) \cong \Sym(n,k)$ and every fiber is diffeomoprhic to $\Sym^+(k)$.
		 \end{theorem}

		Practically, we can now assign, in a smooth manner to a matrix $\Sigma=Q D Q^T$ a class $[Q,D]$ and viceversa. 

		\subsection{Tangent spaces}
		\label{subsec. tangent spaces}
		We now identify the tangent spaces of $M(n,k)$ by means of Proposition \ref{prop. tangent spaces of associated bundle}. In our case, since the left action of $O(k)$ on $\Sym^+(k)$ is given by $L_G S= G S G^T$, we get
		\begin{align*}
			L_A^\ast( S)=&d_t\left(\exp(tA^T)S\exp(tA)\right)\\=&SA-AS\\=&\{S,A\}, \quad A \in \mathfrak{o}(k),\, S \in \Sym^+(k),
		\end{align*}
		where the Lie brackets are denoted via the curly bracket notation $\{\cdot,\cdot\}$ not to make confusion with equivalence classes. Hence, the space that is quotiented out is given by
		\begin{equation*}
			\{QA,-\{S,A\}\,\vert\, A \in \mathfrak{o}(k)\}
		\end{equation*}
		and the equivalence relation is 
		\begin{equation*}
			(V,T)\sim (V+QA,T-\{S,A\})
		\end{equation*}
		so that, recalling that a vector $V \in T_Q \St(n,k)$ can be decomposed uniquely as $V=QA + Q_\perp B, \, A \in \mathfrak{o}(k),\, B \in \Mat(n-k,k)$, we get
		\begin{align*}
			T_{[Q,S]}M=&\{[Q_\perp B, T] \,\vert\,B \in \Mat(n-k,k),T \in \Sym(k)\}\\=&\{(Q_\perp B, T) \,\vert\,B \in \Mat(n-k,k),T \in \Sym(k)\},
		\end{align*}
		where, we identify each class with is \textit{horizontal} component.

		We observe that $\pi_G \circ \sigma: \St(n,k) \times \Sym^+(k) \to \Gr(k,n)$, where $\sigma: \St(n,k) \times \Gr(k,n) \to \Gr(k,n)$ is the canonical projection, is constant on each $O(k)$ orbit $(Q,D)\sim (QG, G^T D G)$. Consequently, it factors out to the projection $\pi_M:\St(n,k) \times_{O(k)} \Sym^+(k)$, i.e., denoting $q: \St(n,k) \times \Sym^+(k) \to \St(n,k) \times_{O(k)} \Sym^+(k)$ the quotient map, we have 
		\begin{equation*}
			\pi_M \circ q=\pi_G \circ \sigma.
		\end{equation*}
		Differentiating on both sides we get
		\begin{equation*}
			d\pi_M([Q,D]) \circ dq(Q,D)= d\pi_G(Q) \circ d\sigma(Q,D).
		\end{equation*}
		Hence, picking any $(V,S) \in T_{Q,D} (\St(n,k) \times \Sym^+(k))$ such that $dq(V,S)=(Q_\perp B, T)$ (again, we identify equivalence classes $[Q_\perp B, T] \sim (Q_\perp B, T)$), we have
		\begin{align*}
			d\pi_M([Q,D])(Q_\perp B, T)= &d\pi_M([Q,D])\circ dq(Q,D)(V,S)\\= &d\pi_G(Q) \circ d\sigma(Q,D)(V,S)\\=&d\pi_G(Q)(V)=B,
		\end{align*}
		where the last equality comes from (\ref{diff of projection in principal bundle}), when we take $[Q \,Q_\perp]$ as a basis of $\R^n$.
		This gives an horizontal-vertical splitting of $T_{[Q,D]} M(n,k)$
		\begin{gather*}
			\cl{V}_{[Q,D]}=\Ker(d\pi_M([Q,D]))=\{(0, T) \,\vert\, T \in \Sym(k)\},\\
			\cl{H}_{[Q,D]}=\{(Q_\perp B, 0) \,\vert\, B \in \Mat(n-k,k)\}.
		\end{gather*}






		\subsection{Pullback metric}
		\label{subsec. metric on sym}



			 We can now compute the differential $d\phi: T_{[Q,D]} M(n,k) \xrightarrow{\cong} T_\Sigma \Sym(n,k)$, where $\Sigma=Q D Q^T$. For that purpose, we select a vector $(Q_\perp B, T) \in T_{[Q,D]_M}$ and take two curves: one $Q(t) \in \St(n,k)$ with $Q(0)=Q$ and $\Dot{Q}(0)=Q_\perp B$ and another $D(t) \in \Sym^+(k)$ with $D(0)=D$ and $\Dot{D}(0)=T$. Differentiating we get
			\begin{align*}
				d\phi_{[Q,D]}((Q_\perp B, T))=&d_t \left(Q(t)D(t)Q(t)^T\right) \big\vert_{t=0}\\=& Q_\perp B D Q^T + Q D B^T Q_\perp^T+ Q T Q^T.
			\end{align*}
			We may check that moving inside an equivalence class does not change the result. Thanks to linearity, it suffices to check that
			\begin{align*}
				d\phi_{[Q,D]}((Q A, \{S,A\}))=&QA S Q^T + Q S (QA)^T Q \{S,A\} Q^T\\=&Q(AS-SA)Q^T+Q(SA-AS)Q^T=0.
			\end{align*}

			 To define a metric on $M(n,k)$ now, it is enough to take the pullback of $g^{BW(n,k)}$ through $\phi$. Calling $h$ the metric on $M(n,k)$ and $V=(Q_\perp B_V,T_V), \, W=(Q_\perp B_W,T_W)$ we get
			\begin{align*}
				h_{[Q,D]}(V,W)=g_{Q D Q^T}^{BW(n,k)}(d\phi_{[Q,D]}(V),d\phi_{[Q,D]}(W)).
			\end{align*}
			Splitting the horizontal and vertical part, we obtain
			\begin{alignat*}{1}
				h_{[Q,D]}^{\hor}(V,W):=&g_{Q D Q^T}^{\hor}(d\phi_{[Q,D]}(V),d\phi_{[Q,D]}(W))\\=&\Tr(Q_\perp Q_\perp^T(Q_\perp B_V D Q^T + Q D B_V^T Q_\perp^T+ Q T_V Q^T)\\\times &Q D^{-1}Q^T(Q_\perp B_W D Q^T + Q D B_W^T Q_\perp^T+ Q T_W Q^T))\\=&\Tr(B_V D B_W^T),
			\end{alignat*}
			and
			\begin{alignat*}{1}
				h_{[Q,D]}^{\ver}(V,W):=&g_{Q D Q^T}^{\ver}(d\phi_{[Q,D]}(V),d\phi_{[Q,D]}(W))\\=&\Tr(QS_{D}(Q^T(Q_\perp B_V D Q^T + Q D B_V^T Q_\perp^T+ Q T_V Q^T)Q)\\ \times &D  S_{D}(Q^T(Q_\perp B_W D Q^T + Q D B_W^T Q_\perp^T+ Q T_W Q^T)Q)Q^T)\\=&\Tr(S_{D}(T_V)D S_D(T_W)).
			\end{alignat*}
			Hence
			\begin{equation*}
				h_{[Q,D]}(V,W)=\Tr(B_V D B_W^T)+\Tr(S_{D}(T_V)D S_D(T_W)).
			\end{equation*}
			Finally, we may check that $h$ is indeed invariant under the action of $O(k)$. This is trivial once we observe that
			\begin{itemize}
				\item for $Q \in \St(n,k)$ and $V \in T_Q \St(n,k)$ the differential of the right $O(k)$ action $QG$ sends $V \mapsto V G$ and, consequently $B_V \to B_V G$,
				\item for $D \in \Sym^+(k)$ and $T \in T_D \Sym^+(k)$ the left $O(k)$ action $G \cdot D= G^T D G$ sends $T \mapsto G^T T G$,
				\item $S_{G^T D G}(G^T T G)=G^T S_D(T) G$. Indeed, let $S =S_D(T)$; we have 
				\begin{align*}
					&G^T D G (G^T S G)+ (G^T S G) G^T D G\\=&G^T (DS+SD) G\\=& G^T T G.
				\end{align*}


			\end{itemize} 



	\subsection{Geodesics}
	\label{subsec. geodesics}

		 We start by recalling the \textit{Koszul formula}. Let $(M,h)$ be a Riemannian manifold. For $p \in M$ and $X, Y \in T_p M$, let $\nabla_X Y$ be the Levi-Civita connection of $(M,h)$ and $\{X,Y\}= \nabla_X Y -\nabla_Y X$ be the Lie bracket. The Koszul formula reads
		\begin{align*}
			2h(\nabla_X Y, Z)=X(h(Y,Z))+ &Y(h(Z,X))- Z(h(X,Y))\\+& h(\{X,Y\}, Z)- h(\{Y,Z\}, X)- h(\{Z,X\}, Y).
		\end{align*}

		 \begin{proposition}
			Let $M(n,k)=\St(n,k) \times_{O(k)}  \Sym^+(k)$. The fibers $M_P(n,k)=\{[Q,S] \,\vert\, \Span(Q)=P\}, \, P \in \Gr(k,n)$ are totally geodesic
		\end{proposition}
		\begin{proof}
			For $U,V$ vertical vector fields and $X$ an horizontal vector field, we want to compute $h(\nabla_U V, X)$ utilizing the Koszul formula. We observe that
			\begin{itemize}
				\item $h(V,X)=h(U,X)=0$,
				\item $\{U,X\}$ and $\{V,X\}$ are vertical, as, for example $d\pi_M(\{U,X\})=\{d\pi_M(U),d\pi_M(V)\}=0$. Also $\{U,V\}$ is vertical, and thus $h(\{U,V\}, X)= h(\{V,X\}, U)=h(\{X,U\}, V)=0$.
				\item $h(U,V)$ is constant along the fibers and hence $X(h(U,V))=d h(U,V)(X)=0$.				
			\end{itemize}
			Putting all together, we get $h(\nabla_U V, X)=0$ and so
			\begin{equation*}
				\cl{H}(\nabla_U V)=0,
			\end{equation*}
			where $\cl{H}(\cdot)$ denotes the horizontal projection.
		\end{proof}
		\begin{remark}
			This is in line with the results found by the authors in \cite{thanwerdas2023bures}. Indeed, in Theorem $5.2$, they state that the geodesic connecting two matrices $\Sigma, \, \Lambda \in \Sym(n,k)$ is unique if and only if $\rk(\Sigma \Lambda)=\rk(\Sigma)$, which means that $\Sigma$ and $\Lambda$ have the same image or, equivalently, belong to the same fiber.
		\end{remark}


		 For the purpose of finding a system of geodesic equations, we study the energy functional and to do that, we need some variational calculus. For every two points $\gamma_0:=[Q_0,D_0], \, \gamma_1:=[Q_1,D_1] \in M(n,k)$ and a smooth curve $\gamma(t):=[Q(t),D(t)] \in M(n,k), \, t\in[0,1]$ with $\gamma(0)=\gamma_0$ and $\gamma(1)=\gamma_1$, we define
		\begin{equation*}
			E(\gamma)=\frac{1}{2}\int_0^1 h(\Dot{\gamma}(t),\Dot{\gamma}(t)) dt.
		\end{equation*}
		A geodesic is then a smooth curve that makes the energy functional stationary under all smooth variations. We work in the bundle coordinates $[Q,D] \in M, \, (Q_\perp B,T) \in T_{[Q,D]} M(n,k)$. The positional coordinates are taken as it is, i.e., $Q \in \St(n,k)$ and $D \in \Sym^+(k)$; the $O(k)$-invariance will be checked later. On the other hand, the horizontal velocity is parametrized freely by $B \in \Mat(n-k,k)$ and the vertical velocity by $T \in \Sym(k)$. In this coordinates, the Lagrangian is given by the kinetic energy
		\begin{align*}
			L(B,D,T):=&\frac{1}{2}h(\Dot{\gamma}(t),\Dot{\gamma}(t))\\=&\frac{1}{2}\Tr(B D B^T)+\frac{1}{2}\Tr(S_D(T)D S_D(T))\\=:&L^{\hor}(B,D,T)+L^{\ver}(B,D,T).
		\end{align*}
		The Euler-Lagrange equations are given by
		\begin{gather*}
			\Dot{(\partial_B L)}=0,\\
			\Dot{(\partial_T L)}-\partial_D L=0,			
		\end{gather*}
		where the dot notation denotes the derivative with respect to $t$.
		Since everything is linear, we may take the variational derivative with respect to every single coordinate separately. For example
		\begin{align*}
			\delta_B \Tr(B D B^T)=& \frac{d}{d\epsilon} \Tr((B+\epsilon\,\delta B) D (B^T+\epsilon\,\delta B^T))\vert_{\epsilon=0}\\=& \Tr(\delta B D  B^T)+ \Tr(B D \delta B^T)\\=& 2 \Tr(\delta B D  B^T),
		\end{align*}
		where all coordinates are understood as functions of $t$. For every matrix function $f:\Mat(p,q) \to \R$ we have
		\begin{equation*}
			\delta f(N)= \sum_{i=1}^p \sum_{j=1}^q \frac{\partial}{\partial n_{ij}}f \delta n_{ij}=\Tr(\delta N (\partial_N f)^T),
		\end{equation*}
		where $\delta N=(\delta n_{ij})$ is an arbitrary small variation of the matrix $N=(n_{ij})$.
		Hence
		\begin{equation*}
			\partial_B L=\partial_B L^{\hor}= B D,
		\end{equation*}
		which gives 
		\begin{equation}
			\label{conservation of momentum}
			B D =K \textrm{ constant.}
		\end{equation}
		Differentating, we get
		\begin{align*}
			\Dot{B}=-B D^{-1}T= -B D^{-1}(D S_D(T)+S_D(T)D).
		\end{align*}
		With respect to $T$, we write
		\begin{align*}
			\delta_T \Tr(S_D(T)D S_D(T))=&\Tr(S_D(\delta T)D S_D(T)+S_D(T)DS_D(\delta T))\\=&\Tr(S_D(\delta T)(D S_D(T)+S_D(T)D))\\=&\Tr(\delta TS_D(D S_D(T)+S_D(T)D))\\=&\Tr(\delta TS_D(T)),
		\end{align*}
		where we have used the fact that $S_D(\cdot)$ is linear and self-adjoint. Thus
		\begin{equation*}
			\partial_{T} L=\partial_{T} L^{\ver}= \frac{1}{2}S_D(T).
		\end{equation*}
		The same strategy readily yields
		\begin{equation*}
			\partial_{D} L^{\hor}=\frac{1}{2}B^T B.
		\end{equation*}
		A little bit more care is needed to compute $\partial_{D} L^{\ver}$. First we defferentiate Sylvester constraint $D S_D(T)+ S_D(T) D=T$ to obtain
		\begin{equation*}
			D \delta_D S_D(T)+ \delta_D S_D(T) D=-\delta D S_D(T)-S_D(T) \delta D,
		\end{equation*}
		and consequently
		\begin{equation*}
		\delta_D S_D(T)=-S_D(\delta D S_D(T)+S_D(T)\delta D).
		\end{equation*}
		This gives
		\begin{align*}
			&\delta_D \Tr(S_D(T)DS_D(T))\\=&\Tr(\delta_DS_D(T)DS_D(T)+S_D(T)\delta DS_D(T)+\delta_D S_D(T)DS_D(T))\\=&\Tr(S_D(T)\delta DS_D(T)+\delta_D S_D(T)(DS_D(T)+S_D(T)D)\\=&\Tr(S_D(T)\delta DS_D(T)-S_D(\delta D S_D(T)+S_D(T)\delta D)(DS_D(T)+S_D(T)D)\\=&\Tr(S_D(T)\delta DS_D(T)-(\delta D S_D(T)+S_D(T)\delta D)S_D(DS_D(T)+S_D(T)D))\\=&\Tr(S_D(T)\delta DS_D(T)-(\delta D S_D(T)+S_D(T)\delta D)S_D(T))\\=&-\Tr(\delta D S_D(T)^2).
		\end{align*}
		Hence
		\begin{equation*}
			\partial_D L^{\ver}=-\frac{1}{2}S_D(T)^2,
		\end{equation*}
		and thus
		\begin{equation*}
			\partial_D L=\frac{1}{2}\left(B^T B -S_D(T)^2\right).
		\end{equation*}
		The Euler-Lagrange equation then reads
		\begin{equation*}
			\Dot{(S_D(T))}=B^T B - S_D(T)^2.
		\end{equation*}
		Calling $S:=S_D(T)$ and
		\begin{align*}
			\mathbf{Q}=&\left[\begin{array}{cc}
				Q&  Q_\perp
			\end{array}\right] \in O(n),\\
			\mathbf{B}=&\left[\begin{array}{cc}
				0&-B^T\\
				B&0
			\end{array}\right]\in \mathfrak{o}(n)=\Skew(n),
		\end{align*} 
		we get the final system
		\begin{align*}
			\begin{cases}
				\Dot{\mathbf{Q}}=\mathbf{Q} \mathbf{B}, \qquad &\mathbf{Q}(0)=\mathbf{Q}_0,\\
				\Dot{D}=D S+ S D,  &D(0)=D_0,\\
				\Dot{B}=-B D^{-1}(DS+SD), \quad  &B(0)=B_0,\\
				\Dot{S}=B^T B -S^2, \quad &S(0)=S_{D_0}(\Dot{D}(0))=S_0.			
			\end{cases}
		\end{align*}
		The first equation $\Dot{\mathbf{Q}}=\mathbf{Q} \mathbf{B}$ translates to the two equations  \begin{gather*}
			\Dot{Q}=Q_\perp B,\\
			\Dot{(Q_\perp)}=-Q B^T
		\end{gather*}
		where the second one is added for the practical purpose of finding $Q_\perp$ that stays consistent with $Q$ over time.
		A straightforward calculation, with the remarks pointed out at the end of the previous subsection, shows that the system is invariant under the $O(k)$ action.


		 \begin{example}
			\label{ex. n=2 k=1}
			Let $n=2$ and $k=1$, then $\St(2,1)\cong S^1$ and $O(k)=\{\pm1\}$ so that $\Gr(1,2)=\R P^1 \cong S^1 /\{\pm 1\}$, wher $\R P^1$ denotes the real projective plane of lines through the origin in $\R^2$. Moreover $\Sym^+(1)=(0,+\infty)$ and consequently, our manifold of study can be characterized as
			\begin{equation*}
				M(2,1)=(S^1 \times (0,+\infty))/\{\pm 1\}, \qquad \pi_M: M(2,1) \to \R P^1,
			\end{equation*}
			with the equivalence relation given by $(q,d) \sim (-q,d)$.The global diffeomorphism is given by
			\begin{equation*}
				\phi: M(2,1) \xrightarrow{\cong} \Sym(2,1), \qquad [q,d] \mapsto dqq^T.
			\end{equation*} 
			If we write
			\begin{equation}
				q(\theta)=\left(\begin{array}{c}
					\cos\theta\\
					\sin\theta
				\end{array}\right), \qquad \theta \in(-\pi,\pi]
			\end{equation}
			and the perpendicular vector
			\begin{equation*}
				q_\perp(\theta)=\left(\begin{array}{c}
					-\sin\theta\\
					\cos\theta
				\end{array}\right)
			\end{equation*}
			we get the bundle and tangent bundel coordinates
			\begin{alignat*}{2}
				&[\theta,d], \qquad &&\theta \in(-\pi,\pi],\, d \in \R,\\
				&(q_\perp(\theta)b,u), \qquad && b,\, u \in \R,
			\end{alignat*}
			where the equivalence class $[\theta,d]$ is modulo the relation $(\theta,d)\sim (\theta+\pi,d)$.

			The solution to the Sylvester equation becomes
			\begin{equation*}
				s:=S_d(t)=\frac{u}{2d}
			\end{equation*}
			and the metric translates to
			\begin{equation*}
				h_{[\theta,d]}(q_\perp b, u)=db^2+\frac{u^2}{4d}=db^2+ds^2.
			\end{equation*}
			The geodesic system transforms to
			\begin{equation}
				\label{ex. geodesic system}
				\begin{aligned}
					\begin{cases}
						\Dot{\theta}=b, \quad &\theta(0)=\theta_0,\\
						\Dot{d}=2ds,\quad &d(0)=d_0,\\
						\Dot{b}=-bd^{-1}(2ds)=-2bs,\quad &b(0)=b_0,\\
						\Dot{s}=b^2-s^2,\quad &s(0)=s_0.
					\end{cases}
				\end{aligned}
			\end{equation}
			From  (\ref{ex. geodesic system}), we immediately gather that the fibers are totally geodesic. Indeed $b_0=0$ implies $b \equiv 0$ and consequently $\Dot{\theta} \equiv 0$; the system reduces to
			\begin{equation*}
				\begin{cases}
					\Dot{d}=2ds,\\
					\Dot{s}=-s^2,
				\end{cases}
			\end{equation*}
			which gives the closed form geodesics
			\begin{equation*}
				d(t)=d_0(1+s_0t)^2.
			\end{equation*}
			We observe that this equation matches the equation found by the authors in \cite{thanwerdas2023bures}, Theorem $4.2$ applied to the case $\Sym^+(1)$.

			On the other hand, if $b_0 \neq 0$ we can still solve the system. Define $r=s/b$ and compute 
			\begin{equation*}
				\Dot{r}=\frac{\Dot{s}b-\Dot{b}s}{b^2}=b(r^2+1)
			\end{equation*}
			and
			\begin{equation*}
				\Dot{b}=-2b^2r.
			\end{equation*}
			Dividing
			\begin{align*}
				\frac{db}{dr}=\frac{\Dot{b}}{\Dot{r}}=-\frac{2rb}{r^2+1}
			\end{align*}
			or equivalently
			\begin{equation*}
				\frac{d \ln b}{dr}=-\frac{2r}{r^2+1},
			\end{equation*}
			which, integrating, gives
			\begin{equation}
				\label{ex. b r relationship}
				b(r^2+1)=b_0(r_0^2+1)=:C,\quad \textrm{constant,}
			\end{equation}
			where $r_0=s_0/b_0$.
			It follows that $r$ grows linearly $r(t)=r_0+Ct$ and thus by (\ref{ex. b r relationship})
			\begin{equation*}
				b(t)=\frac{C}{(Ct+r_0)^2+1},
			\end{equation*}
			and 
			\begin{equation*}
				s(t)=r(t)b(t)=\frac{C(Ct+r_0)}{(Ct+r_0)^2+1}=\frac{C^2t+s_0(r_0^2+1)}{(Ct+r_0)^2+1}.
			\end{equation*}
			Since (\ref{conservation of momentum}) gives $bd=b_0d_0$, we get
			\begin{equation}
				\label{ex. d equation}
				d(t)=d_0\frac{(Ct+r_0)^2+1}{r_0^2+1}.
			\end{equation}
			Lastly, a straightforward integration of $\Dot{\theta}=b$ yields
			\begin{equation*}
				\theta(t)=\theta_0+\left(\arctan\left(Ct+r_0\right)-\arctan\left(r_0\right)\right).
			\end{equation*}
			We see that geodesics with horizontal initial velocity ($s_0=0$) do not enjoy the same nice qualities of vertical geodesics. The last equation in (\ref{ex. geodesic system}) shows that the vertical acceleration $\Dot{s}$ depends on the hori
			zontal velocity $b$. Geometrically, this is due to the horizontal metric being dependent on the vertical configuration coordinate $d$. 

			Finally, we check whether the geodesic equations found match the ones computed by the authors in \cite{thanwerdas2023bures}. To start, the diffeomorphism $\phi$ can be expressed in terms of the $(\theta,d)$ coordinates as
			\begin{equation*}
				[\theta,d]\mapsto dq(\theta)q(\theta)^T=d\left(\begin{array}{cc}
					\cos^2\theta&\cos\theta\sin\theta\\
					\cos\theta\sin\theta&\sin^2\theta
				\end{array}\right),
			\end{equation*}
			which is indeed a bidimensional rank one covariance matrix. 
			Moreover, expressing $q(\theta(t))$ in the basis $\{q(\theta_0),q_\perp(\theta_0)\}$ we get
			\begin{align*}
				q(\theta(t))=&\cos(\theta(t)-\theta_0)q(\theta_0)+\sin(\theta(t)-\theta_0)q_\perp(\theta_0)\\=&\cos(\theta(t)-\theta_0)q_0+\sin(\theta(t)-\theta_0)w_0,
			\end{align*}
			where, and henceforth, for simplicity of notatipon we denote $q_0:=q(\theta_0)$ and $w_0.=q_\perp(\theta_0)$.
			Using cosine and sine addition formulas and the fact that $\cos(\arctan x)=(1+x^2)^{-1/2}$ and $\sin(\arctan x)=x(1+x^2)^{-1/2}$ we get
			\begin{gather*}
				\begin{aligned}
					\cos(\theta(t)-\theta_0)=&\cos(\arctan r(t)-\arctan r_0)\\=&\frac{1+r(t)r_0}{\sqrt{(1+r(t)^2)(1+r_0^2)}},
				\end{aligned}\\
				\begin{aligned}
					\sin(\theta(t)-\theta_0)=&\sin(\arctan r(t)-\arctan r_0)\\=&\frac{r(t)-r_0}{\sqrt{(1+r(t)^2)(1+r_0^2)}}.
				\end{aligned}
			\end{gather*}
			By (\ref{ex. d equation}), we get
			\begin{equation*}
				\sqrt{d(t)}=\sqrt{d_0}\sqrt{\frac{1+r(t)}{1+r_0}},
			\end{equation*}
			so that
			\begin{alignat*}{2}
				\sqrt{d(t)}q(\theta(t))=&\sqrt{d_0}\sqrt{\frac{1+r(t)}{1+r_0}}\bigg(\cos(\arctan r(t)-\arctan r_0)q_0 \\&+ \sin(\arctan r(t)-\arctan r_0)w_0\bigg)\\=& \sqrt{d_0}\left(\frac{1+r(t)r_0}{1+r_0^2}q_0+\frac{r(t)-r_0}{1+r_0^2}w_0\right)\\=&\sqrt{d_0}\left(\left(1+\frac{r_0Ct}{1+r_0^2}\right)q_0+\frac{Ct}{1+r_0^2}w_0\right)\\=&\sqrt{d_0}\left(\left(1+s_0 t\right)q_0+b_0 t \, w_0\right)\\=&
				\sqrt{d_0}q_0+ \sqrt{d_0}(s_0 q_0+b_0 w_0)t.
			\end{alignat*}
			This gives a geodesic equation on the manifold $\Sym(2,1)$
			\begin{align*}
				\Sigma(t)=&d(t)q(\theta(t))q(\theta(t))^T\\=&\left(\sqrt{d_0} q_0+ \sqrt{d_0}(s_0 q_0+b_0 w_0)t\right)\\ & \times\left(\sqrt{d_0}q_0+ \sqrt{d_0}(s_0 q_0+b_0 w_0)t\right)^T\\=&d_0 q_0 q_0^T+ d_0\left(2s_0 q_0q_0^T+b_0\left(q_0 w_0^T+w_0 q_0^T\right)\right)t\\&+d_0\left(s_0^2 q_0 q_0^T+b_0^2 w_0 w_0^T +s_0b_0\left(q_0 w_0^T+w_0 q_0^T\right)\right)t^2
			\end{align*}
			that matches the equation found by the authors in \cite{thanwerdas2023bures} for the special case $n=2$, $k=1$ once we notice that
			\begin{gather*}
				d_0q_0q_0^T=\Sigma(0)=\phi([\theta_0,d_0]),\\
				\vspace{10pt}\\			
				\begin{aligned}
					d\phi_{[\theta_0,d_0]}(w_0b_0,u_0)=&u_0q_0q_0^T+d_0b_0\left(q_0 w_0^T+w_0 q_0^T\right)\\=&d_0\left(2s_0 q_0q_0^T+b_0\left(q_0 w_0^T+w_0 q_0^T\right)\right).
				\end{aligned}
			\end{gather*}
		\end{example}
		With some additional effort, we can generalize Example \ref{ex. n=2 k=1} to the case $n \geq 2$, $k=1$.
		\begin{example}
		 	 Set $n \in \bb{N}$ and $k=1$. The geometric structure carries over verbatim, from the previous example, to
		 	\begin{equation*}
		 		M(n,1)=S^{n-1} \times _{\{\pm1\}} (0, +\infty), \qquad \pi_M:M(2,1) \to \R P^{n-1};
		 	\end{equation*}
		 	the coordinates become $([q,d],b,u)$ with $q \in S^{n-1}$, $d \in (0,+\infty)$, $b \in \R^{n-1}$, $u \in \R$ and the metric simplifies to
		 	\begin{equation*}
		 		h_{[q,d]}(q_\perp b, u)=d\|b\|^2+ds^2.
		 	\end{equation*}
		 	The geodesic equations transforms to
		 	\begin{equation*}
		 		\begin{cases}
		 			\Dot{\mathbf{q}}=\mathbf{q} \mathbf{b}, \quad & \mathbf{q}(0)=\mathbf{q}_0\\
		 			\Dot{d}=2ds,\quad &d(0)=d_0,\\
		 			\Dot{b}-2bs,\quad &b(0)=b_0,\\
		 			\Dot{s}=\|b\|^2-s^2,\quad &s(0)=s_0,
		 		\end{cases}
		 	\end{equation*}
		 	where
		 	\begin{align*}
		 		\mathbf{q}=&[\begin{array}{cc}
		 			q &q_\perp
		 		\end{array}]\in O(n), \\
		 		\mathbf{b}=&\left[\begin{array}{cc}
		 			0&-b^T\\
		 			b&0
		 		\end{array}\right] \in \mathfrak{o}(n)=\Skew(n).
		 	\end{align*}
		 	Defining $r=s/\|b\|$, a computation similar to Example \ref{ex. n=2 k=1} yields
		 	\begin{equation*}
		 		\frac{d}{d r}\|b\|=\frac{-2r}{r^2+1}
		 	\end{equation*}
		 	and thus
		 	\begin{gather*}
		 		\|b\|(r^2+1) =\Tilde{C}:=\|b_0\|(r_0^2+1),\\
		 		b(r^2+1)= \mathbf{C}:=b_0(r_0^2+1).
		 	\end{gather*}
		 	$r(t)=r_0+\Tilde{C}t$ is therefore linear and all other solution equations follow as above
		 	\begin{gather*}
		 		b(t)=\frac{\mathbf{C}}{1+r(t)^2},\\
		 		s(t)=\|b(t)\|r(t)=\frac{\Tilde{C}r(t)}{1+r(t)},\\
		 		d(t)=d_0\frac{1+r(t)^2}{1+r_0^2}.
		 	\end{gather*}
		 	To compute $q$, we first observe that since $\Dot{b}=-2sb$ and $s \in \R$ is a scalar, $b$ has constant direction or equivalently
		 	\begin{equation*}
		 		\hat{b}=\frac{b}{\|b\|} \textrm{ is constant.}
		 	\end{equation*}
		 	Therefore equation $\Dot{\mathbf{q}}=\mathbf{q b}$ can be written
		 	\begin{equation}
		 		\label{ex2. q equation}
		 		\Dot{\mathbf{q}}(t)=\mathbf{q}(t)\hat{\mathbf{b}}\|b(t)\|
		 	\end{equation}
		 	with
		 	\begin{equation*}
		 		\hat{\mathbf{b}}:=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
		 			0&-\hat{b}^T\\
		 			\hat{b}&0
		 		\end{array}\right].
		 	\end{equation*}
		 	Equation (\ref{ex2. q equation}) readily integrates to
		 	\begin{equation}
		 		\label{ex2. q sol}
		 		\mathbf{q}(t)=\mathbf{q}_0 \exp(\hat{\mathbf{b}}\theta(t)),
		 	\end{equation}
		 	with
		 	\begin{align*}
		 		\theta(t):=&\int_0^t \|b(\tau)\| d\tau\\=&\int_0^{r(t)}\frac{d\rho}{\rho^2+1}\\=&\arctan r(t)-\arctan r_0.
		 	\end{align*}		 	
		 	Since $\hat{\mathbf{b}}^3=-\hat{\mathbf{b}}$, rearranging gives
		 	\begin{align*}
		 		\exp(\hat{\mathbf{b}}\theta(t))=&I +\sin\theta(t)\hat{\mathbf{b}}+(1-\cos\theta(t))\hat{\mathbf{b}}^2\\=&\left[\begin{array}{cc}
		 			\cos\theta(t)& -\hat{b}\sin\theta(t)\\
		 			\hat{b}^T\sin\theta(t)& I_{n-1}-(\cos\theta(t)-1)\hat{b}\hat{b}^T
		 		\end{array}\right],
		 	\end{align*}
		 	where the last equality comes from
		 	\begin{equation*}
		 		\hat{\mathbf{b}}^2=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
		 			-1 & 0\\
		 			0 & -\hat{b} \hat{b}^T
		 		\end{array}\right].
		 	\end{equation*}
		 	With this, the first column of the right-hand side of (\ref{ex2. q sol}) reads
		 	\begin{equation*}
		 		q(t)=\cos\theta(t)q_0+\sin\theta(t)w_0
		 	\end{equation*}
		 	where $w_0:=q_\perp (0)\hat{b}$.
		 \end{example}
		 In more general cases, we can resort to numerical calculations to obtain geodesic solutions and plots.
		 \begin{example}
		 	Let $n=5$ and $k=3$. Let 
		 		\begin{alignat*}{2}
		 			&\mathbf{Q}_0=I_n, \qquad &&B_0=\frac{1}{2}\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
		 				1&0&0\\
		 				0&1&0
		 			\end{array}\right]\\
		 			&D_0=I_3 &&T_0=\frac{1}{2}I_3
		 		\end{alignat*}
		 	so that
		 	\begin{equation*}
		 		S_0=S_{D_0}(T_0)=\frac{1}{4}I_3.
		 	\end{equation*}
		 	Solving numerically the system we get the graphs shown in fig. [\ref{ex3. Q fig}]-[\ref{ex3. S fig}], where the entries not plotted are constant and equal to their starting value.
		 	\begin{figure}[H]
		 		\centering
		 		\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{Qplot.pdf}
		 		\caption{Time evolution of the entries of $Q$.}
		 		\label{ex3. Q fig}
		 	\end{figure}

		 	\begin{figure}[H]

		 		\centering
		 		\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{Dplot.pdf}
		 		\caption{Time evolution of the entries of $D$.}
		 		\label{ex3. D fig}
		 	\end{figure}

		 	\begin{figure}[H]

		 		\centering
		 		\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{Bplot.pdf}
		 		\caption{Time evolution of the entries of $B$.}
		 		\label{ex3. B fig}
		 	\end{figure}

		 	\begin{figure}[H]

		 		\centering
		 		\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{Splot.pdf}
		 		\caption{Time evolution of the entries of $S$.}
		 		\label{ex3. S fig}
		 	\end{figure}
		 	With these simple initial conditions the system can be translated again to a system of one dimensional equations and solved with techniques similar to the previous examples. Indeed denoting $b_i=B_{ii}, \, d_i=D_{ii}$ and $s_i=S_{ii}$ we get for $i=1,2$
		 	\begin{equation*}
		 		\begin{cases}
		 			\Dot{d}_i=2s_i d_i, \quad d_i(0)=1,\\
		 			\Dot{b}_i=-2s_i b_i, \quad b_i(0)=\frac{1}{2},\\
		 			\Dot{s}_i=b_i^2-s_i^2, \quad s_i(0)=\frac{1}{4}.
		 		\end{cases}
		 	\end{equation*}
		 	which is solved by
		 	\begin{equation*}
		 		\begin{cases}
		 			d_i=\frac{4}{5}(1+r^2),\\
		 			b_i=\frac{5}{8(1+r^2)},\\
		 			s_i=\frac{5r}{8(1+r^2)},
		 		\end{cases}
		 	\end{equation*}
		 	where
		 	\begin{equation*}
		 		r(t)=\frac{1}{2}+\frac{5}{8}t.
		 	\end{equation*}
		 	For $i=3$ we get the simplified system
		 	\begin{equation*}
		 		\begin{cases}
		 			\Dot{d}_3=2s_3 d_3, \quad d_3(0)=1,\\
		 			\Dot{s}_3=-s_3^2, \quad s_3(0)=\frac{1}{4},
		 		\end{cases}
		 	\end{equation*}
		 	which is readily solved by
		 	\begin{equation*}
		 		d_3(t)=(1+\frac{1}{4}t)^2, \qquad s_3(t)=\frac{1/4}{1+\frac{1}{4}t}.
		 	\end{equation*}
		 	One can show that, denoting $\theta(t)=\arctan(r(t))-\arctan(1/2)$ and $R(\theta)=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
		 		\cos\theta&-\sin\theta\\
		 		\sin\theta&\cos\theta
		 	\end{array}\right]$, we get
		 	\begin{equation*}
		 		\mathbf{Q}(t)=\diag(R(\theta(t)),R(\theta(t)),1)
		 	\end{equation*}
		 	and
		 	\begin{gather*}
		 		Q_{11}(t)=Q_{22}(t)=\cos\theta(t),\\
		 		Q_{41}(t)=Q_{52}(t)=\sin\theta(t)
		 	\end{gather*}
		 	while all other entries of $Q(t)$ stay constant.

		 	The numerical machinery can be used to obtain results in cases with more complicated initial conditions. For example, let $\mathbf{Q}_0$ and $D_0$ be as before and
		 	\begin{gather*}
		 		 B_0=\frac{1}{2}\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
		 			1&1/2&-2/5\\
		 			-3/5&1&0
		 		\end{array}\right]\\
		 		T_0=\frac{1}{2}\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
		 			3/10&-7/10&2/5\\
		 			1&-1/2&1/5\\
		 			-1&1&0
		 		\end{array}\right]
		 	\end{gather*}
		 	so that
		 	\begin{equation*}
		 		S_0=S_{D_0}(T_0)=\frac{1}{4}T_0.
		 	\end{equation*}
		 	In this case, closed form solutions are difficult to obtain; however we can numerically integrate the system to obtain approximate solutions. The graphs are plotted in fig. [\ref{ex3. Q fig1}]-[\ref{ex3. S fig1}].
		 	\begin{figure}[H]

		 		\centering
		 		\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{Qplot1.pdf}
		 		\caption{Time evolution of the entries of $Q$.}
		 		\label{ex3. Q fig1}
		 	\end{figure}

		 	\begin{figure}[H]

		 		\centering
		 		\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{Dplot1.pdf}
		 		\caption{Time evolution of the entries of $D$.}
		 		\label{ex3. D fig1}
		 	\end{figure}

		 	\begin{figure}[H]

		 		\centering
		 		\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{Bplot1.pdf}
		 		\caption{Time evolution of the entries of $B$.}
		 		\label{ex3. B fig1}
		 	\end{figure}

		 	\begin{figure}[H]

		 		\centering
		 		\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{Splot1.pdf}
		 		\caption{Time evolution of the entries of $S$.}
		 		\label{ex3. S fig1}
		 	\end{figure}
		 \end{example}



		\subsection{Logarithm bijection}
		\label{subsec. bijection}
		 From \cite{bendokat2024grassmann}, section $5$ and \cite{thanwerdas2023bures}, Theorem $5.3$, we notice that the number of logarithms, and hence of energy-minimizing geodesics, of $\Gr(k,n)$ endowed with the Frobenius metric and those of $\Sym(n,k)$ endowed with the Bures-Wasserstein metric is the same. Indeed, in \cite{bendokat2024grassmann}, the authors link the number of minimizing geodesics between two points $P_1, P_2 \in \Gr(k,n)$ to the number $r$ of orthogonal principal angles between $P_1$ and $P_2$. In particular the logarithms are indexed by set $I_1 = O(r)$ (see \cite{bendokat2024grassmann} Theorem $5.5$). On the other hand, in \cite{thanwerdas2023bures}, the authors link the number of minimizing geodesics between two matrices $\Sigma_1, \, \Sigma_2 \in \Sym(n,k)$ to the difference between $k$ and $l:=\rk(\Sigma_1 \Sigma_2)=\rk(X^T Y)$, where $X X^T = \Sigma_1$ and $Y Y^T =\Sigma_2$. In this case, the authors give the index set 
		\begin{align*}
			I_2=&\{R \in O(k) | X^T Y R  \in Cov (k)\}\\=&\{R \in O(k) | X^T Y R=(X^T \Sigma_2 X)^{1/2}\}.
		\end{align*}
		Assuming $P_1=\Span(Q_1)$ and $P_2=\Span(Q_2)$ and letting $\Sigma_1=Q_1 D_1 Q_1^T$ and $\Sigma_2=Q_2 D_2 Q_2^T$, since $\lambda_i(Q_1^T Q_2)=\cos\theta_i$, where $\lambda_i(Q_1^T Q_2)$ denotes the $i$-th singular value of $Q_1^T Q_2$ and $\theta_i$ denotes the $i$-th principal angle between $P_1$ and $P_2$, then
		\begin{equation*}
			\rk(\Sigma_1 \Sigma_2)=\rk(Q_1^T Q_2)=\#\{i \,\vert\,\theta_i \neq \pi/2\}
		\end{equation*}
		and hence $k-l=r$.
		\begin{proposition}
			Let $X , \, Y \in \Mat(n,k)^\ast$, call $l=\rk(X^T Y)$ and $r=k-l$. Then there exists a bijection between the two index sets
			\begin{gather*}
				I_1 = O(r) \textrm{ and}\\
				I_2=\{R \in O(k) | X^T Y R =(X^T Y Y^T X)^{1/2}\}.
			\end{gather*}
		\end{proposition}
		\begin{proof}
			Since $\rk(X^T Y)=l$, we can perform the singular value decomposition
			\begin{equation*}
				X^TY = U D V^T,
			\end{equation*}
			with $ U, \, V \in O(k)$,and $D=\diag(\sigma_1,\dots,\sigma_l,0,\dots,0)$. In the same basis we have
			\begin{equation*}
				(X^T Y Y^T X)^{1/2}= U D U^{T}.
			\end{equation*}
			This forces 
			\begin{equation*}
				R=V \left[\begin{array}{cc}
					I_l &0 \\
					0 & R_{r}
				\end{array}\right] U^T, \quad R_{r} \in O(r).
			\end{equation*}
		\end{proof}
		This bijection can be readily carried over to the lenght minimizing geodesics of $\Sym(n,k)$ and $\Gr(k,n)$. This suggests that there should be a link between the geometry of the fixed rank covariance matrices and that of the Grassmann manifold that could be investigated further.






