% In an ideal non-mixing case (\(g_r =0.2\)), the mixing entropy is expected to be zero, as particle positions remain fixed. However, due to small system size effects and bin discretization constraints, we observe a systematic offset ($\delta=0.5$) in mixing entropy. This arises because finite bin sizes result in occasional misclassification of particle types, introducing artificial fluctuations. While these effects diminish for larger systems, in our finite-sized simulations, this leads to a nonzero baseline in mixing entropy for the solid phase. Using \(\delta\) as the baseline, the mixing entropy fo the completely mixed case will be \(\ln(3) + \delta\). Therefore baselines for \(\delta\) and \(\ln(3) + \delta\) are included to account for the error arising due to the small system size (see Fig.~\ref{FIG.main3}(b)), when compared the mixing behavior across various gradients and parameters. } 

The mixing entropy as a function of the gradient is shown in Fig.~\ref{FIG.main3}(c), with vertical dashed lines marking the critical transition points defined as 10\% of the maximum saturated entropy and 10\% of the minimum entropy (see Fig.~\ref{FIG.main3}(d)).
% Mixing entropy as a function of the gradient is shown in Fig.~\ref{FIG.main3}(c), with vertical dashed lines marking the critical transition points—defined as 10\% of the maximum saturated entropy and 10\% of the minimum entropy.
%This systematic bias should be considered when interpreting entropy values, particularly in comparisons across different regimes.} 
We found that for a low gradient, the cluster is in a fluid regime in Fig.~\ref{FIG.main3}(c), characterized by high particle mobility and uniform mixing. In contrast, for high gradients, the cluster is in a non-mixing, solid-like regime, where particles exhibit minimal movement and maintain rigid configurations. This fluid and solid type behaviors are visually demonstrated in Fig.~\ref{FIG.main3}(e) and Fig.~\ref{FIG.main3}(g), and further captured in Supplementary Videos M3 and M5 respectively, which depict the time evolution of the color configuration within the clusters. The figures also denote the velocity vectors of each particle, further emphasizing the differences in dynamical behavior between these regimes.

% The Intermediate regime represented in Fig.~\ref{FIG.main3}(f), lies between these extremes. It showcases partial mixing and transitional behavior. 
In between the two vertical lines of Fig.~\ref{FIG.main3}(c), defined by the mixing entropy (Fig.~\ref{FIG.main3}(d)), we find a regime where the clusters deform and break up (Fig.~\ref{FIG.main3}(f) ; Supplementary Video M4.
We can therefore categorize the system into three distinct regimes: the fluid state characterized by complete mixing, the solid state with no mixing, and the intermediate breaking regime, which exhibits partial mixing and cluster break up. These phases and the breaking instability will be the focus of our study, but we first describe the surprising "solid" phase which appears in our model for strong gradients.
