\subsection{Comparison of Fluid and Solid Type Phases.} 
% The solidification regime occurs under extreme gradients where outwardly radiating forces act symmetrically (see Fig.~\ref{FIG3}), creating a balanced force field that locks the cluster into a fixed, compact configuration. This transition is characterized by strong radial alignment, minimal internal rearrangements, and reduced degrees of freedom. The resulting rigid structures reflect a loss of entropy, signaling a shift to an ordered state. Translational motion is suppressed, and migratory capabilities are significantly diminished. This regime highlights how external forces impose stability and rigidity, stopping the dynamic behavior observed in lower-gradient regimes.
