\subsection{ Forward Migration Analysis Reveals Optimal Range of Chemoattractant Gradient}
To quantify how effectively a cluster aligns its movement with an external chemical gradient, we calculated the Forward Migration Index (FMI), which
% To investigate how clusters respond to external chemical gradients, we calculated the \textbf{Forward Migration Index (FMI)} for clusters.
is defined as the ratio of the distance traveled by the cluster in the direction of the gradient to the total distance traveled. 
% This metric provides a quantitative measure of how effectively a cluster aligns its movement with an external chemical gradient. 
For gradients in the breaking regime, the FMI was calculated for clusters before they broke up. The FMI showed an initial linear increase with the gradient, followed by high fluctuations and a decline at very high gradient values (Fig.~\ref{FIG.main3}(h)). The initial linear increase in FMI suggests an optimal gradient range where clusters respond linearly to the externally applied gradient. This range is crucial because it represents a regime where clusters can most efficiently sense and respond to chemical signals. Such behavior is indicative of a balance between the driving gradient forces and the intrinsic cluster dynamics, enabling coordinated movement. Beyond this range, the observed fluctuations and decline in FMI may reflect the inability of clusters to maintain cohesion or directional migration under excessively strong gradients. %High fluctuations and a further decay in FMI were observed with increasing gradient strength.



% \textbf{FMI Increases with Cluster Size}
%The observed dependence of FMI on gradient strength suggests that both external forces and intrinsic cluster properties play a role in determining migration efficiency. To further dissect this relationship, 
Larger aggregates tend to have a higher ability to sense and respond to chemical signals more effectively \cite{malet-engraCollectiveCellMotility2015, copenhagen2018frustration}. This higher migratory efficiency can be attributed to the larger total force due to the larger chemotactic force difference across the diameter of the larger clusters \cite{copenhagen2018frustration}, which enable them to maintain directional movement despite environmental fluctuations. To quantify this trend, we examined how cluster size influences response dynamics under varying gradients in our model. A heatmap of FMI as a function of size and gradient (see Fig.~\ref{FIG.main3}(i)), reveals that larger clusters exhibit more effective migration than smaller ones at low gradients, as one might expect. 
%Heatmaps demonstrate that at low gradients, larger clusters migrate more effectively than smaller ones (also see Fig.~\ref{FIG.SI2}.
Indeed, the trend of the size dependence of FMI in the breaking regime for $g_r = 0.006$ (see Fig.~\ref{FIG.SI2}) aligns closely with the observed size dependence of FMI in the experiments (see Supplementary Figure~SI2($D^{ii}$) of \cite{malet-engraCollectiveCellMotility2015}).
% Notably, also the velocity of clusters remains nearly constant across all sizes for $g_r = 0.006$ (also see Fig.~\ref{FIG.SI2}(b)), which aligns with the experimental system (see Panel:(0-100) of Figure~3B of \cite{malet-engraCollectiveCellMotility2015})
% Heatmaps demonstrate that at low gradients, larger clusters migrate more effectively than smaller ones (also see Fig.~\ref{FIG.SI2}). 
 Interestingly, in our model, this trend continues at the high gradients of the solid phase, despite the overall decrease in the average speed of the clusters (Fig.~\ref{FIG.main3}(j)). 
% With increasing chemical gradient, the cluster enters a gradient-dominated regime where the gradient force becomes the primary factor governing its behavior. In this state, the outwardly radiating gradient applies equal and opposing forces on all sides of the circular cluster, creating a balanced force field. This uniform distribution of forces locks the cluster into a fixed configuration, restricting internal rearrangements and shape changes. As a result, the cluster transitions into a solid-like state, losing its fluidity and becoming structurally rigid. This solidification significantly reduces the cluster’s ability to migrate or respond dynamically to further increases in the gradient. The emergence of this gradient-dominated state underscores how external forces can stabilize cluster structures while suppressing their adaptive and migratory capabilities.\\

