Our work highlights an intriguing feedback-driven breakup instability in chemotactic lymphocyte clusters exposed to intermediate chemoattractant gradients (Fig.~\ref{FIG.main5}(a, b)). We showed that, beyond a threshold gradient, clusters elongate perpendicular to the gradient and eventually fragment into smaller clusters. This instability is driven by a feedback mechanism where the front-edge cells move persistently up the gradient while cells toward the sides slip forward and flatten the leading edge, breaking the cluster’s symmetry. The high curvature sides then experience increased persistent motion outwards, stretching the cluster laterally until it breaks (Fig.~\ref{FIG.main6}). 
%Breakup frequency rises with gradient but stabilizes in high-gradient regimes, where normal forces mitigate fragmentation (Fig.~\ref{FIG.main5}(c)). Size-dependent breakup patterns (Fig.~\ref{FIG.main5}(d), (e)) show larger clusters are more prone to instability under intermediate gradients. Experimental and simulation probability distributions confirm these trends, emphasizing the role of cluster size in determining stability thresholds.
Consistently, we found that the probability of breakup rises with gradient strength and with cluster size (Fig.~\ref{FIG.main5}(d), (e)), indicating a size-dependent instability threshold that disrupts cluster cohesion and limits cluster size.
% \cite{}

From a physics perspective, this behavior can be viewed in the context of the process of equilibrium phase separation due to attractive adhesion interactions between particles which is disrupted by activity, that has been studied in different synthetic systems ranging from colloids to active nematics \cite{aparna_2013reentrant, tayar2023}. However, in our system, the activity is not uniform and its influence is coupled to the boundary shape.  One can also consider extending this concept to the context of motility-induced phase separation (MIPS) in active matter systems, where, in the absence of external cues, self-propelled particles with purely repulsive interactions can spontaneously phase-separate into dense clusters and dilute gas-like regions \cite{cates2015motility,sanoria_influence_2021,sanoria_percolation_2022}. Such coarsening would typically produce a single large cluster at steady state. However, in our chemotactic system the external gradient provides a directed feedback that produces an instability that causes fragmentation and counteracts indefinite coarsening which may be observable in appropriately designed active colloidal systems \cite{dauchot2019interrupted}. %It is interesting to compare this to an analogous phenomenon seen in certain synthetic active colloid systems where aligning active colloids undergo fast clustering and initial phase separation, but the process is interrupted when large clusters start breaking, preventing complete phase separation \cite{dauchot2019interrupted}.

