\section{Introduction}

In October 2021, the Cyberspace Administration of China released draft regulations that sparked public outcry and drew intense international media attention \citep{reuters2022weibo, nytimes2022internet}. These regulations required online service providers to ``prominently display IP address locations of user accounts,'' specifying that platforms must show the province for domestic users and the country or region for overseas users. While the Chinese government justified the policy as a measure to curb online misinformation and counter foreign influence, it drew criticism for further restricting freedom of speech for Chinese internet users. On March 17, 2022, less than a month after Russia began its invasion of Ukraine, Sina Weibo, China’s largest microblogging platform, revealed users' IP locations in accordance with the new regulations. Weibo added a location tag to each post or comment related to the Russo-Ukrainian war, claiming it aimed to minimize misinformation and ensure content authenticity. However, many believe the real intent was to restrict overseas users from sharing information that contradicted the government's stance.\footnote{For example, Weibo's announcement highlights a user's IP location in Ukraine, subtly insinuating foreign users as potential purveyors of misinformation. In a later press conference, A speaker from the Taiwan Affairs Office of the State Council even made the purpose of the policy clear: ``to pinpoint and neutralize individuals from Taiwan who could be intentionally stirring the pot in cross-strait relations.'' See \url{http://www.gwytb.gov.cn/m/speech/202205/t20220511_12435159.htm}. Accessed on May 2024.} At noon on April 28, Weibo abruptly disclosed each user’s location on every post and comment. Other major Chinese social media companies, including Douyin, Toutiao, Zhihu, Kuaishou, and Xiaohongshu, soon followed suit with similar policies. By the end of 2022, all social media platforms in China had adopted the user's IP disclosure policy.

This paper examines the causal impact of the location disclosure policy implemented on Weibo. Authoritarian regimes use a range of tools to manage online discourse, including censorship, surveillance, and increasingly, user information disclosure. Prior work shows that Chinese authorities remove content linked to collective action while tolerating some criticism \citep{king2013censorship, lorentzen2014strategic, roberts2018censored}. These cyberspace regulations deter participation through both targeted repression and ambient monitoring \citep{esarey2011digital, pan2023disguised}. In addition, social media platforms like Weibo function as tools for state messaging, information gathering, and controlled public engagement \citep{chen2017authoritarian, chen2017information, qin2017freer}. With platform cooperation, the state has adopted user-level labeling tools—such as real-name registration and location disclosure—to steer user behavior without direct censorship. These policies make identity attributes visible to others and can change user behavior. Despite their growing adoption, empirical evidence on the behavioral effects of such policies remains limited, largely due to data and identification challenges. A smaller body of research suggests that visible identity cues—like regional tags—can heighten social divisions and incivility \citep{peng2021amplification, chen2021science, yu2024topic, guo2023civilizing}. However, little is known about their causal impact on user behavior and online discourse.

Studying the effects of user information disclosure presents several empirical challenges. First, data accuracy is a central concern. In heavily censored environments like Weibo, post-censorship data can bias inference—changes in critical comments may reflect shifts in censorship intensity rather than actual behavior. Prior work shows that around 3\% of sensitive posts are removed within 30 minutes and 90\% within 24 hours~\citep{king_how_2013}, while non-sensitive posts remain online, creating survivorship bias. Public datasets, such as Weiboscope and Weibo-Cov \citep{weiboscope, weibocov}, often amplify this issue: many rely on crawling schemes that oversample high-follower accounts or are assembled post-censorship using narrow keyword lists. Moreover, basic metadata, such as user location, often rely on users’ self-designated tags, which may introduce systematic geographic error~\citep{qin_media_2018}. These limitations make it difficult to construct samples that reliably capture authentic user behavior.

Second, measurement is often limited. Most studies using existing Weibo datasets retain only original posts and high-level reposts \citep[e.g.,][]{lu2021public, qin_social_2024, meng_spreading_2025}, omitting detailed engagement data such as the volume and content of engagement, and nested threads—where much of the deliberation, conflict, and behavioral response to government policies takes place. Without such data, it is difficult to analyze how conversations evolve within comment sections. Moreover, researchers often lack information about which users are subject to information disclosure and what specific details are revealed, making it difficult to identify who is treated.

Third, causal identification remains challenging. To our knowledge, no experimental studies exist, in part due to ethical constraints.  Observational studies often rely on platform-generated signals such as deletion flags or user tags, but these are governed by opaque criteria and applied unevenly. Without knowing how the platform selects users for disclosure, unobserved confounding is a serious concern. Most datasets rely on low-frequency scraping and do not track accounts or posts over time, which limits their usefulness for studying behavioral dynamics over time.

We address these empirical challenges through a real-time, high-frequency data collection strategy that continuously monitors a set of prominent government-affiliated Weibo accounts and their comment sections. This approach yields a highly granular, longitudinal dataset at both the account and post levels. By scraping data roughly every five minutes, we capture most comments before they are censored or removed, minimizing the influence of post-publication moderation. In addition to post-level engagement metrics, we track top comments and their replies, which are ranked by the platform primarily based on likes and responses. This allows us to observe how discussions evolve and how the top comment section updates over time. The dataset spans a sudden, unannounced policy change, enabling credible before-and-after comparisons using an interrupted time series design. Its longitudinal structure supports within-account comparisons across the policy threshold, while repeated scraping of the first-page comment section captures user discourse over an extended period.

\section{Data and Measurement}

We collaborated with industry experts to design a real-time data collection system that monitored 165 prominent Weibo accounts affiliated with the Chinese government. These include official accounts of government offices and state media outlets—such as newspapers, television stations, and major media outlets—identified in a series of reports on institutional influence in 2021.\footnote{These reports include multiple editions of Government Weibo Influence Reports (政务微博影响力报告), Sina Government Weibo Reports (新浪政务微博报告), and Weibo Trending Topics Data Reports (微博热搜榜数据报告). See Table~\ref{table:account_list} in the SM for a full list of accounts we collected data from.} We focused on these accounts because, following the government’s 2013 crackdown on key opinion leaders \citep{pan2025disguised}, the comment sections of these accounts—due to high traffic and visibility—have become important spaces for public expression and policy discussion. These accounts reach large audiences and maintain high engagement, making them well-suited for observing shifts in public sentiment. Our aim is to capture both the content posted by these accounts and the real-time public engagement their posts generate. 

