\subsection{Location disclosure deepened regional divisions and reduced participation on local issues}

Why did domestic users, particularly those commenting on regions other than their own, become less engaged in discussions of local issues after the introduction of IP location disclosure? Our analysis suggests that the policy reshaped both the interpretation and perceived risks of online expression. By making users’ provincial origins visible, it increased the salience of regional identity and transformed comment sections into sites of heightened intergroup sensitivity. This change not only altered the tone of discourse but also deterred participation—especially from out-of-province users.

Previously, critical comments on local issues could appear anonymous or broadly directed. After location disclosure, they became traceable to specific locations. As a result, even generic remarks were easily interpreted as attacks from outsiders. Figure~\ref{fig:qualititive_comment_2} in the SM provides an example. A Fujian-based user, commenting on population decline in the northeast, invokes the stereotype that ``mafia is everywhere'' in Heilongjiang, Liaoning, and Jilin. The visible location tag invites retaliation: users target Fujian in return, citing regional tropes about fraud and scams. What might have been a passing remark now escalates into interprovincial antagonism.

To assess whether this dynamic was isolated or systemic, we examine second-tier replies across posts using an LLM to detect regionally discriminatory language. Figure~\ref{fig:division2} shows a sharp rise in such language after location disclosure in the replies to front-page comments. Moreover, as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:decomposed_regional_discrimination} in the SM, this increase is driven almost entirely by cross-province exchanges; within-province interactions remain stable. These patterns indicate that location disclosure deepened existing regional divisions, raising the social and reputational cost of participating in public discussions about other regions.

This dynamic helps explain the sharp decline in comment activity on posts about local issues discussed earlier. As users became more aware of each other’s regional identity, the risk of being targeted with regionally discriminatory language increased—not only for those posting comments but also for those considering whether to reply. When even neutral or critical remarks could trigger interprovincial backlash, users likely became more hesitant to participate, especially in discussions involving other provinces. These conflicts reduced the likelihood that comments would attract replies, even when they remained visible.  Figure~\ref{fig:comment_reply} reflects this shift: after the introduction of location disclosure, a smaller share of comments received responses, indicating thinner and more cautious engagement on the social media platform.

Moreover, we quantify the decline in user interaction by measuring the rotation among first-page comments using the CoV of comment floor numbers—a proxy for the dynamism of the comment section. Figure~\ref{fig:cov} shows a clear drop in CoV following the implementation of the location disclosure policy. As location tags heightened the risk of regional backlash, users became more reluctant to engage, especially on posts involving other provinces. This led to slower turnover among top comments and a more static comment hierarchy, consistent with a broader chilling effect on interaction.

In sum, user location disclosure reconfigured both the meaning and the risks of online discourse. By making regional identity salient, it raised the cost of participation—especially across provincial boundaries—leading to reduced comment volume, lower dissent, and thinner engagement.


\subsection{Robustness checks}

We conduct a series of robustness checks, detailed in the SM, to verify the stability of our main findings on the decline in comment volume due to the implementation of the user location disclosure policy. First, we vary the bandwidths used in the local smoothing, including those selected by the \texttt{rdrobust} package, bandwidths chosen via cross-validation, and a range of fixed windows (8, 24, 72, 96, and 120 hours). In all cases, the observed decline in comment volume remains consistent. Second, we re-estimate the models using a log-transformed outcome, $\log(\#comments + 1)$, to assess sensitivity to outliers. While the main analyses use the number of unique comments to accommodate posts with zero comments, the log transformation yields qualitatively similar results. Finally, we perform placebo tests by shifting the timing of the policy intervention to alternative dates. These placebo specifications show no discontinuities, reinforcing that the observed effects are indeed tied to the actual timing of the location disclosure policy.


\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}

We investigate the impact of a user location disclosure policy on Sina Weibo, a prominent social media platform in China. Although the policy seemed aimed at deterring engagement by overseas users, it had little impact on their actual participation. Instead, it suppressed domestic users’ engagement, particularly in commenting on out-of-province issues. The decline appears to be driven by heightened exposure to regional discrimination, as location tags made users more vulnerable to backlash when commenting on issues beyond their home provinces.

Our analyses show that authoritarian regimes can suppress criticism and shape online discourse by leveraging existing social divisions. Following the location disclosure mandate on Weibo, regional tensions among users intensified the constraints on expression. As conversations about local affairs grow more insular and uniform, the regime may become less able to identify emerging discontent—undermining its responsiveness and the long-term quality of governance \citep{chen2017information}. 

