Parallel algorithms also assume that $G$ is known including the edge weights. %The only difference is parallel algorithms try to reduce the runtime complexity making multiple processors work in parallel to solve the problem faster. % instead of a single centralized processor.  

The (message-passing) distributed computing model (which we simply call the message-passing model throughout this paper hereafter) lifts the assumption of  known $G$. It only considers that each node (processor) knows its neighboring processors and the weights of the communication links (edges) connecting it to those neighboring processors. Further, only an $O(\log n)$-size message (the $\mathcal{CONGEST}$ model) can be sent on each edge in each time step. 
For computing MST in this model, as the output, every processor knows which of its adjacent edges belong to MST. %, i.e., the processors collectively keep the information about the MST. %It is easy to see that the centralized and parallel algorithms do not easily extend to construct MST in the distributed computing model. %For example, the centralized algorithms, Prim's and Kruskal's, require processing one node at a time (for the sake of not introducing cycles), making it difficult to make them run in parallel. Additionally, the centralized algorithms assume to know the state of the whole graph. %, which is difficult to discover while only knowing the neighbors. 
