$O(\sqrt{n}\log ^{*}n+D)$, where $D$ is the diameter of $G$. Furthermore, the best-known time  lower bound is 
$\Omega (\sqrt {n}/{\log n}+D)$ \cite{PelegR00}. These bounds were achieved considering the synchronous setting (also ours) in which each processor performs its operations synchronously with all others. % and hence time can be measured in the number of rounds.


In this paper, we initiate the study of MST construction using mobile agents (aka robots). Suppose we are given $n$ agents  positioned initially arbitrarily on the nodes of $G$. Any algorithm run by agents should output  an MST such that (i) each of the $n$ agents is positioned on a different node of $G$ and (ii) an agent positioned at a node knows which of its adjacent edges are the MST edges. 

The agent model has two major differences with the message-passing model.
\begin{itemize}
\item [I.] In the agent model, agents but not the graph nodes are assumed to have memory and processing power, whereas the message-passing model assumes each graph node is a processor having both unrestricted memory and processing power. 
\item [II.] In the agent model, an agent positioned at a graph node does not have message send/receive capability to/from an agent positioned at its neighboring node, whereas a processor (at a node) in the message-passing model has message send/receive capability to/from a processor positioned at its neighbor node. 
\end{itemize}
Difference II is problematic for the agent model since  if an agent (at a node) needs to deliver a message to its neighbor(s), it has to relocate to that neighbor and there has to be an agent positioned on that neighbor. %, otherwise the message cannot be delivered since both sender and receiver must be at the same node to be able to have the message delivered. %deliver information. %are at the same node and now they can exchange information).
%Given these differences, it is easy to see that MST construction is difficult in the agent model compared to the distributed computing model and due to the nature of communication, the time complexity of MST construction in the agent model seems to be higher than the distributed computing model.  We show, in this paper, that MST construction is indeed possible in the agent model and provide a series of algorithms focusing on time complexity as well as memory requirement at each agent.  

Let us discuss why it is important to study MST in the agent model. First, since the agent model is different from the message-passing model, it demands new techniques for computation. Second, 
the agent model has been gaining significant attention recently in computing.  For example, Pattanayak {\it et al.} \cite{PattanayakBCM24} considered computing Maximal Independent Set (MIS) of $G$.
Chand {\it et al.} \cite{ChandMS23} considered computing small dominating sets of $G$.  Both of these results assume that graph parameters (such as $n$ and $\Delta$) are known to agents a priori. %, except for the configurations where all agents start initially from the single node. %, they . %They do not assume a priori information about $n,\Delta$.
Triangle counting by agents is studied and applied to other problems in Chand {\it et al.} \cite{AgentTriangleCounting}. 
Given these recent developments, MST is a natural, fundamental problem to  investigate in the agent model.


