There is a vast literature on MST construction in centralized, parallel, and message-passing models. In the centralized computing model, the first algorithm for MST is due to  Bor\r{u}vka \cite{GrahamH85} %. Bor\r{u}vka's algorithm  
proceeds in stages. In each stage, it identifies a forest $F$ consisting of the MOE incident to each vertex in $G$, then forms the graph $G_1 = G \backslash F$ as the input to the next step. %Here G \ F denotes the graph derived from G by contracting edges in F (by the Cut property, these edges belong to the MST). 
Each stage takes $O(m)$ time and the number of vertices remaining to be processed is reduced by half in each step. Therefore, this algorithm constructs MST in $O(m \log n)$ time.
Prim's algorithm \cite{Dijkstra59} %This algorithm 
grows the MST one edge at a time. The algorithm initially starts from a vertex in $G$ which is selected arbitrarily.  In each step, the MST is augmented with a MOE, say $(x,y)$, such that node $x$ is already in MST but not node $y$. % is not yet in the MST. %It was shown that the MST grows in each step, i.e., if an edge is added to MST in a step then it remains in the MST thereafter. 
This algorithm finishes in time $O(m \log n)$ using binary heap and in time $O(m + n \log n)$ using Fibonacci heap. 
Kruskal's algorithm \cite{Kruskal1956} also grows the MST at most one edge at a time. %This algorithm first sorts all edges in increasing order of their edge weights. After that, 
It picks the smallest edge at each step and adds it to MST if it does not create a cycle in the MST constructed so far. %  and checks if the new edge creates a cycle in the MST constructed (MST is initialized empty in the first step) up to the previous step. If it does not form the cycle, then that edge is included in MST, and discarded otherwise.
%The algorithm repeats until it includes $n - 1$ edges in MST.
This algorithm finishes in $O(m \log n)$ time.
The reverse-delete algorithm, a reverse of Kruskal's algorithm \cite{Kruskal1956}, finishes in $O(m \log n (\log \log n)^3)$ time. 
All these algorithms are greedy. Later studies focused on improving runtime \cite{Chazelle00a,KargerKT95}. 

In the parallel computing model, with $O(n)$ processors, \cite{BaderC06,ChongHL01,PettieR02} show that MST can be computed in $O(\log n)$ time.
