\subsection{Parallel preconditioning}
Usually the resulting linear system (\ref{kron_ode_source}) is large and ill-conditioned, and solving it is a core problem in the application of BVMs. If a direct method is employed to solve such a linear system, the operation cost can be very high for practical application. Therefore interest has been turned to iterative solvers, such as the GMRES method. As we know that a clustered spectrum often translates in rapid convergence of GMRES method \cite{Saad86}, so we use the GMRES method for solving the resulting linear system (\ref{kron_ode_source}). 
Firstly, we establish the following preconditioner 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{P} = \omega(A)\otimes I_m - \tau I_N\otimes D,
\end{equation}
where the $\omega$-circulant approximation $\omega(\cdot)$ introduced for LMM in boundary value form to integrate PDEs in \cite{Bertaccini03}. When we set $\omega = \exp{(\mathrm{i}\theta)}$ with $\theta\in(-\pi, \pi]$, the following decomposition holds:
\begin{equation}
\omega(A) = \Theta^{*}F^{*}\Lambda_\omega F\Theta,
\end{equation}
where $\Theta = {\rm diag}\left(1,\omega^{-1/n},\cdots,\omega^{-(N-1)/N}\right)$, $\Lambda_\omega$ is a diagonal matrix containing the eigenvalues of $\omega(A)$ and $F$ is the discrete Fourier matrix. According to the property of Kronecker product, we can factorize $\mathcal{P}$ as follow:
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{P} = \left(\Theta^{*}F^{*}\otimes I_m\right)(\Lambda_\omega\otimes I_m - \tau I_N\otimes D)(F\Theta\otimes I_m),
\end{equation*}
thus the application ${\bm z} = \mathcal{P}^{-1}\widetilde{{\bm r}}$ needed by the preconditioned GMRES method is computed with three steps:
\begin{itemize}
    \item[a)] Use the FFT and solve a diagonal system to compute $\widetilde{{\bm v}}_1 = \left(\Theta^{*}F^{*}\otimes I_m\right)^{-1}\widetilde{{\bm r}}$;
    \item[b)] Solve the block diagonal system $(\Lambda_\omega\otimes I_m - \tau I_N\otimes D)\widetilde{{\bm v}}_2 = \widetilde{{\bm v}}_1$, which is equivalent to solving $N$ auxiliary linear systems 
    \begin{equation}
     (\lambda_j I - \tau D)\widetilde{{\bm v}}_{2,j} = \widetilde{{\bm v}}_{1,j},~~\Lambda_{\omega} = diag(\widetilde{\lambda}_1,\cdots,\widetilde{\lambda}_N),\quad j = 1,2,\cdots,N,
     \label{sec4.19}
    \end{equation}
    which can be solved in parallel fashion, see Appendix $C$ for details.
    \item[c)] Use the inverse FFT and solve a diagonal system to compute $\widetilde{{\bm z}} = \left(F\Theta\otimes I_m\right)^{-1}\widetilde{{\bm v}}_2$.
\end{itemize}

The spectrum of the preconditioned system is clustered around $(1, 0)\in\mathbb{C}$ and hence the convergence of the GMRES method can be faster if the underlying preconditioner $\mathcal{P}$ is used. By using arguments similar to those used in \cite{cosine_family_general}, we obtain the following result.
\begin{theorem}
\label{themeig}
If the block $\{\omega\}$-circulant preconditioner $\mathcal{P}$ is such that $\omega =\exp(\mathrm{i}\theta), \theta = \pi$ and $\Re(\lambda_r(D)) \leq 0,~r = 1,\cdots,m$, then the eigenvalues of the preconditioned matrix $\mathcal{P}^{-1}\mathcal{M}$ are equal to $1$ except for at most $2mk$ outliers.
\end{theorem}

Theorem \ref{themeig} shows that the preconditioned GMRES method will terminate within $2mk +1$ iterations, while diagonalizability of $\mathcal{P}^{-1}\mathcal{M}$ may help us to estimate the 
rate of convergence. In fact, the preconditioned matrix can be easily written as low-rank and small-norm perturbations of the
identity matrix, it is possible to use the method in \cite{Carr23} for investigating the convergence of the GMRES method applied to such a linear system, we leave it for our future work. However, in these cases it is not obvious whether the preconditioned matrix is diagonalizable, nor when we can expect convergence in fewer steps because of eigenvalue clustering.
