\section{Numerical examples}
\label{sec5}
In this section, we present numerical experiments for three representative fractional PDE models to illustrate the performance of the proposed GMM time integrator. The models considered are: (i) a pure half-diffusion equation (sometimes called a ``fractional heat” model), (ii) a fractional mass transfer (diffusion) model with an added reaction term, and (iii) an advection-dominated fractional transport model (advection–diffusion with fractional dynamics). Unless stated otherwise, all simulations are carried out in one spatial dimension on the domain $\Omega=(0,20)$ with uniform grid spacing $h$.
Spatial operators are discretised with second-order central differences.
For case (iii) periodic boundary conditions are imposed, giving the circulant matrices
\[-\Delta \;\approx\; -\frac{1}{h^{2}}
\begin{pmatrix}
-2 & 1 & &  & 1\\
1 & -2 & 1 &  & \\
& \ddots& \ddots & \ddots &  \\
 & & 1 & -2 & 1\\
1 &  & & 1 & -2
\end{pmatrix},
\qquad
\mathcal{D} \;\approx\; \frac{1}{2h}
\begin{pmatrix}
0 & 1 & & & -1\\
-1 & 0& 1 & & \\
 & \ddots& \ddots & \ddots &  \\
 & &-1 & 0 & 1 \\
1 & & & -1& 0 
\end{pmatrix}.\]


Time integration is carried out from $T=0$ up to $T=20$ using the second order GMM \eqref{A_a}-\eqref{B_b} posed in section \ref{sec4}. A circulant preconditioner is employed in the solver for the larger linear systems (especially in the advection dominated case) to improve efficiency. The results demonstrate the accuracy and stability of the GMM scheme (with and without the preconditioner) across all three models. We now discuss each example in detail.

\subsection{The half-diffusion equation ($\mathcal{L} = 0$)}
We first consider the pure half-diffusion model, in which transport is driven solely by the half-Laplacian:
\begin{equation}
   \left\{ \begin{aligned}
        \frac{\partial u}{\partial t}&= -\varepsilon(-\Delta)^{\frac{1}{2}}u+f(x,t)~~~\text{in}~ \Omega,\\
        u &= 0~~~\text{on}~\RR\backslash\Omega.
    \end{aligned}\right.\label{heat}
\end{equation}
Here $\varepsilon>0$ is the anomalous diffusivity and $(-\Delta)^{1/2}$ captures long-range jumps or sub-diffusive spreading typical of Lévy-flight processes, highly heterogeneous media, or visco-elastic trapping.  The source term $f(x,t)$ represents external injection or removal of mass. We choose the diffusion coefficient $\varepsilon = 0.1$ and test the homogeneous case with the initial condition $u(x) = \frac{1}{(1+x^2)^2}$, the numerical diffusion process is presented in Figure \ref{heat_sol}. And in Figure \ref{converge_0}, we compare the convergence order for the GMM with and without the preconditioner. By choosing $f(x,t) = -\varepsilon\cos(t)  \frac{  x^4 + 6x^2 - 3 }{2 \left( x^2 + 1 \right)^3} - \frac{\sin(t)}{\left( x^2 + 1 \right)^2}$ with the same initial condition $u_0(x)$ and same $\varepsilon$ we have the exact solution $u(x,t) = \frac{\cos(t)}{(1+x^2)^2}$. 

