\subsection{An advection dominated equation with half-diffusion ($\mathcal{L} = \delta \mathcal{D}$)}
Finally, we turn to a half-diffusion model with advection, which describes one-dimensional, nonstationary mass transfer in which a systematic bias or flow is superimposed on jump-driven diffusion. Compared with the standard diffusion  $\Delta$, the half Laplacian $-(-\Delta)^{\frac12}$ provides much weaker diffusion. The advection term retains its classical first-order time dependence, while the absence of a source term ($f=0$) indicates that there is no absorption or release of substance during transport. The equation is given by: 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial u}{\partial t}= -\varepsilon(-\Delta)^\frac{1}{2}u + \delta \frac{\partial u}{\partial x}+ f(x,t),~ u,f\in L_{\text{per}}^2(\mathbb{T}_{2L})\label{diffusion_ux}
\end{equation}

 In this section, we take \(\delta = 0.2\) and \(\varepsilon = 0.01\) over a relatively long time period of \(T = 20\).  This is an advection-dominated case.  In this advection-dominated regime, convective transport is the primary mechanism, carrying the material predominantly in one direction. Half diffusion here plays a secondary role, capturing subdiffusive effects that slightly smooth out the concentration profile and thus, it cannot smooth the solution as rapidly as the Laplacian $\Delta$ can. Under these conditions standard central schemes may generate non-physical oscillations at sharp fronts or discontinuities because of a high Péclet number \cite{peclet}. In our tests such oscillations appeared near the boundary as the final time $T$ increased, but they disappeared relative quickly when the spatial grid was refined. As same we did in previous two simulations, we provide the numerical convergence in the Figure \ref{converge_ux}, the performance of preconditioned and standard BVM are compared as well. And in Figure \ref{sol_ux} displays the computed evolution of (\ref{diffusion_ux}) without the source $f(x,t)$, the solution is smooth and free of oscillations. In contrast to the two earlier models, whose eigenvalues lie only on the real axis, the eigenvalues of the matrix $D$ derived in (\ref{diffusion_ux}) spread out in a more complicated pattern, as shown in Figure \ref{eig_ux}, this helps explain why many LMMs fail.

