\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec7}
In summary, we have introduced a framework for handling evolutionary PDEs containing half-diffusion terms by transforming the original problem into a second-order damped equation and then splitting it into two first-order PDEs in time. This spectrum doubling reformulation avoids direct computation of the singular integral defining the half-Laplacian, thereby simplifying both analysis and computation. We showed that commonly used time integrators, including various LMMs and Runge–Kutta schemes, can exhibit stability issues for this class of problems. To address this, we proposed the application of BVMs, which use boundary conditions at both the initial and final steps. These methods preserve second-order convergence and offer unconditional stability, as confirmed by their eigenvalue-based stability analysis. Moreover, to tackle the resulting large and ill-conditioned linear systems, we discussed iterative solvers and appropriate preconditioners, showing that the spectrum clustering of the preconditioned systems leads to efficient convergence. Numerical experiments validated the theoretical findings, underscoring the method’s reliability for both diffusion- and advection-dominated scenarios. Looking ahead, we plan to extend this work to two-dimensional domains and investigate more involved problems, such as the fractional Schrödinger equation, where the half-Laplacian remains a challenge.


