\section{Appendix}\label{se:appendix}

\subsection*{Q1: How can I perform an induction proof for binary decision diagrams?}

\textbf{A:} To perform an induction proof involving Binary Decision Diagrams (BDDs), you typically use mathematical induction on a structural property (e.g., number of variables or size of the BDD). Here's a generic outline:

\begin{enumerate}[label=\textbf{Step \arabic*:}]
    \item \textbf{Base Case:} Prove the property holds for a trivial BDD, such as one with 0 or 1 variable.

    \item \textbf{Inductive Hypothesis:} Assume the property holds for all BDDs with up to \( n \) variables.

    \item \textbf{Inductive Step:} Prove it holds for \( n+1 \) variables. Use the fact that BDDs for \( n+1 \) variables can be split at the root into two BDDs with at most \( n \) variables each (Then/Else branches). Apply the inductive hypothesis.
\end{enumerate}

You might use this to prove correctness, size bounds, or structural properties of BDDs.

\subsection*{Q2: How does this work for the function \( f = x_1x_2 + x_3x_4 + \cdots + x_{2n-1}x_{2n} \)?}

\textbf{A:} Consider \( f_n = \sum_{i=1}^{n} x_{2i-1}x_{2i} \). Each term is a 2-variable AND. With a fixed variable ordering \( x_1 < x_2 < \dots < x_{2n} \), the BDD can be constructed incrementally.

\subsection*{Inductive Structure:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Base Case (\( n = 1 \)): \( f_1 = x_1 x_2 \), BDD has 3 internal nodes.
    \item Inductive Hypothesis: BDD for \( f_k \) has size \( \leq 3k + 1 \).
    \item Inductive Step: Add \( x_{2k+1}x_{2k+2} \). New BDD adds 3 nodes. Total becomes \( 3(k+1) + 1 \).
\end{itemize}

Thus, the size is linear in \( n \).

\subsection*{Q3: How does it work for \( f = x_1x_2x_3 + x_4x_5x_6 + \cdots + x_{n-2}x_{n-1}x_n \)?}

\textbf{A:} Let \( n = 3k \), and define \( f_k = \sum_{i=1}^{k} x_{3i-2} x_{3i-1} x_{3i} \).

\subsection*{Inductive Strategy:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Base Case: \( f_1 = x_1x_2x_3 \), BDD has 3 internal nodes.
    \item Inductive Step: Add new term \( x_{3k+1}x_{3k+2}x_{3k+3} \), which adds 4 nodes (including the OR node).
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Conclusion:}
The BDD size grows linearly in \( k \). So for \( n = 3k \), the BDD size is \( O(n) \).

\subsection*{Q4: Can you also prove by induction that BDDs for totally symmetric functions are polynomially bounded?}

\textbf{A:} Yes. Let \( f_n \) be a totally symmetric Boolean function: it depends only on the Hamming weight of its input. We'll show the size of its BDD is \( O(n^2) \).

\subsection*{Inductive Argument:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Base Case (\( n = 1 \)): BDD size is constant.
    \item Inductive Hypothesis: Assume \( f_n \) has a BDD with size \( \leq Cn^2 \).
    \item Inductive Step: Construct BDD for \( f_{n+1} \). At each level \( i \), track how many ones have been seen (\( 0 \leq k \leq i \)). Each level has \( i+1 \) nodes.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Node Count:}
\[
\sum_{i=0}^{n} (i+1) = \frac{(n+1)(n+2)}{2} = O(n^2)
\]

